window.QDATA_5 = [{"id":"hg4","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 2F Q1 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart shows the numbers of cups of coffee sold in a shop on Friday: espresso 40, americano 45, mocha 20, cappuccino 30, latte not yet shown. (a) How many cups of mocha were sold? (b) The shop sold 25 cups of latte. Draw a bar on the bar chart to show this information. (c) The shop sold more cups of espresso than cups of cappuccino. How many more?","answer":"(a) 20 cups\n(b) Draw a latte bar at height 25\n(c) 10 more","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Reading the mocha bar directly from the chart: 20 cups.","(b) Draw a bar for latte reaching up to 25 on the vertical axis.","(c) Espresso (40) − cappuccino (30) = 10 more cups."]},{"id":"hg5","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 1F Q2 - Pictogram","q":"The pictogram shows the number of pizzas sold in a shop on three days, where a full circle represents 12 pizzas and a half-circle represents 6 pizzas. Sunday: 3 full + 1 half. Monday: 2 full. Tuesday: 6 full + 1 half. (a) How many pizzas were sold on Sunday? (b) More pizzas were sold on Tuesday than on Monday. How many more? (c) 30 pizzas were sold on Wednesday. Complete the pictogram for Wednesday.","answer":"(a) 42 pizzas\n(b) 54 more\n(c) 2 full circles + 1 half circle","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Sunday = 3 full + 1 half = 3×12 + 6 = 42 pizzas.","Monday = 2×12 = 24 pizzas. Tuesday = 6 full + 1 half = 6×12 + 6 = 78 pizzas.","(b) Tuesday − Monday = 78 − 24 = 54 more pizzas.","(c) 30 pizzas ÷ 12 = 2.5 circles, so draw 2 full circles and 1 half circle for Wednesday."]},{"id":"hg6","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 1F Q8 - Two-Way Table","q":"Kai sells drinks of coffee, tea and hot chocolate. On Monday, Kai sold a total of 140 drinks: 58 coffee, 36 tea, the remainder hot chocolate. On Tuesday, Kai sold a total of 160 drinks: 70 tea, 50 hot chocolate, the remainder coffee. Use this information to complete the two-way table (rows Monday, Tuesday, Total; columns Coffee, Tea, Hot chocolate, Total).","answer":"Monday: coffee 58, tea 36, hot chocolate 46, total 140. Tuesday: coffee 40, tea 70, hot chocolate 50, total 160. Column totals: coffee 98, tea 106, hot chocolate 96, grand total 300","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Monday hot chocolate = 140 − 58 − 36 = 46.","Tuesday coffee = 160 − 70 − 50 = 40.","Column totals: coffee = 58+40 = 98, tea = 36+70 = 106, hot chocolate = 46+50 = 96.","Grand total = 98+106+96 = 300, which matches 140+160 = 300."]},{"id":"hg7","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 1F Q18 - Estimated Mean from a Table","q":"The table gives information about the number of kilometres that Ted cycled on each of the 30 days in April. 0≤K<5: frequency 8. 5≤K<10: frequency 7. 10≤K<15: frequency 3. 15≤K<20: frequency 10. 20≤K<25: frequency 2. Calculate an estimate for the mean number of kilometres that Ted cycled on each day in April.","answer":"Mean = 11 km","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Use the midpoint of each class: 2.5, 7.5, 12.5, 17.5, 22.5.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 2.5×8 + 7.5×7 + 12.5×3 + 17.5×10 + 22.5×2 = 20 + 52.5 + 37.5 + 175 + 45 = 330.","Total frequency = 8+7+3+10+2 = 30.","Estimated mean = 330 ÷ 30 = 11 km."]},{"id":"hg8","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 1F Q21 - Mean, Median and Range","q":"e, f and g represent integers such that e, f, 7, g, 12, 17 is a list of integers written in order of size. The integers have a range of 15, a median of 8.5, and a mean of 9. Work out the value of e, the value of f and the value of g.","answer":"e = 2, f = 6, g = 10","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Range = 15: the largest value is 17, so 17 − e = 15, giving e = 2.","Median of 6 numbers = average of 3rd and 4th values = (7 + g)/2 = 8.5, so g = 10.","Mean = 9, so the sum = 6 × 9 = 54.","Sum = e + f + 7 + g + 12 + 17 = 2 + f + 7 + 10 + 12 + 17 = 48 + f = 54, so f = 6.","Check order: 2, 6, 7, 10, 12, 17 is correctly in ascending order."]},{"id":"hg9","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 2F Q2 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart shows the weight of wheat, in millions of tonnes, produced by each of five countries in 2021: Denmark 4, Egypt 9, Turkey 18, Sweden 3, Morocco 7.5, UK not yet shown. The UK produced 14 million tonnes of wheat. (a) Draw a bar on the bar chart to show this information. (b) Write down the weight of wheat produced by Turkey. (c) One of these countries produced 7.5 million tonnes of wheat. Which country? (d) One of these countries produced three times as many million tonnes of wheat as Sweden. Which country?","answer":"(a) Draw UK bar at 14\n(b) Turkey = 18 million tonnes\n(c) Morocco\n(d) Egypt","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Draw a bar for the UK reaching up to 14 on the vertical axis.","(b) Reading directly from the chart, Turkey produced 18 million tonnes.","(c) Morocco's bar shows 7.5 million tonnes.","(d) Three times Sweden's amount = 3 × 3 = 9 million tonnes, which matches Egypt."]},{"id":"hg10","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 1F Q2 - Pictogram","q":"The pictogram shows the number of tyres sold in a garage each month from January to April, where a full circle represents 8 tyres. January: 3 full. February: 4 full. March: 5 full + 1 quarter. April: 3 full + 1 half. (a) How many tyres were sold in January? (b) Work out the total number of tyres sold in the four months from January to April. (c) In May, 22 tyres were sold. Show this information on the pictogram.","answer":"(a) 24 tyres\n(b) 126 tyres\n(c) 2 full circles + 3-quarter circle","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) January = 3 × 8 = 24 tyres.","February = 4 × 8 = 32. March = 5 full + 1 quarter = 5×8 + 2 = 42. April = 3 full + 1 half = 3×8 + 4 = 28.","(b) Total = 24 + 32 + 42 + 28 = 126 tyres.","(c) May = 22 tyres ÷ 8 = 2.75 circles, so draw 2 full circles and a 3/4 circle (2×8+6=22)."]},{"id":"hg11","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 1F Q9 - Pie Chart","q":"Tamsin asked some people how they prefer their eggs cooked: scrambled, boiled, poached or fried. The pie chart shows scrambled = 6 people at 72°, boiled at 132°, poached at an unknown angle, fried at 48°. (a) Use the pie chart to complete the table (find the frequency for boiled, the frequency and angle for poached, and the frequency for fried). (b) One of these people is selected at random. Write down the probability that this person chose scrambled.","answer":"(a) boiled = 11, poached frequency = 9 at 108°, fried = 4\n(b) P(scrambled) = 1/5","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Scrambled: 6 people = 72°, so 1 person = 12°, meaning the total number of people = 360° ÷ 12° = 30.","Boiled = 132° ÷ 12° = 11 people. Fried = 48° ÷ 12° = 4 people.","Poached angle = 360 − 72 − 132 − 48 = 108°, so poached = 108° ÷ 12° = 9 people.","Check: 6+11+9+4 = 30. ✓","(b) P(scrambled) = 6/30 = 1/5."]},{"id":"hg12","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 2F Q11 - Two-Way Table and Probability","q":"The children at a holiday club play sport either on Monday or on Tuesday, choosing tennis, athletics or cricket. The two-way table shows: Monday tennis 29, Monday cricket 58, Monday total 110; Tuesday row incomplete; column totals tennis 62, cricket 105, grand total 240. (a) Complete the two-way table. (b) One of the children who plays cricket is chosen at random. Write down the probability that this child plays cricket on Monday.","answer":"(a) Monday: tennis 29, athletics 23, cricket 58, total 110. Tuesday: tennis 33, athletics 50, cricket 47, total 130. Totals: tennis 62, athletics 73, cricket 105, grand total 240\n(b) P = 58/105","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Monday athletics = 110 − 29 − 58 = 23.","Tuesday tennis = 62 − 29 = 33. Tuesday cricket = 105 − 58 = 47.","Tuesday total = 240 − 110 = 130, so Tuesday athletics = 130 − 33 − 47 = 50.","Athletics column total = 23 + 50 = 73. Check: 62+73+105 = 240. ✓","(b) P(Monday | cricket) = 58/105 (58 played cricket on Monday out of 105 who played cricket in total)."]},{"id":"hg13","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 2F Q18 - Range, Median and Mode","q":"Rio has six cards. He writes a number on each card so that the range of the numbers is 10, the median of the numbers is 7.5, and the mode of the numbers is 6. Rio arranges the cards in order of size: [ ], [ ], 6, [ ], 10, 14. Three of the numbers are hidden. Complete the cards to show the three numbers that are hidden.","answer":"The hidden numbers are 4, 6, 9 (so the full list is 4, 6, 6, 9, 10, 14)","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Range = 10: the largest value is 14, so the smallest value is 14 − 10 = 4 (first card).","Median of 6 numbers = average of 3rd and 4th values = (6 + 4th value)/2 = 7.5, so the 4th value = 9.","Mode = 6, so 6 must appear more than once. Since it only appears once so far (3rd card), the 2nd card must also be 6.","Full ordered list: 4, 6, 6, 9, 10, 14. Check: range = 14−4 = 10 ✓, median = (6+9)/2 = 7.5 ✓, mode = 6 ✓."]},{"id":"hg14","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 1F Q3 - Pictogram","q":"The pictogram gives information about the number of kilometres Thomas travelled on Monday, Tuesday, Wednesday and Thursday, where a full square represents 4 kilometres. Monday: 4 full. Tuesday: 4 full. Wednesday: 4 full + 1 three-quarter square. Thursday: 3 full. (a) Write down the number of kilometres Thomas travelled on Tuesday. (b) On Wednesday, Thomas travelled further than he did on Thursday. How much further? (c) On Friday, Thomas travelled 13 kilometres. Show this information on the pictogram.","answer":"(a) 16 km\n(b) 7 km further\n(c) 3 full squares + 1 quarter square","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Tuesday = 4 × 4 = 16 km.","Wednesday = 4 full + 3/4 square = 16 + 3 = 19 km. Thursday = 3 × 4 = 12 km.","(b) 19 − 12 = 7 km further.","(c) Friday = 13 km ÷ 4 = 3.25 squares, so draw 3 full squares and a 1/4 square."]},{"id":"hg15","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 2F Q3 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart shows the area, in thousands of hectares, used to grow cucumbers in each of four countries in 2021: Mexico 20, Spain 8, Japan 11, Egypt 21, Norway not yet shown. (a) Write down the number of hectares used to grow cucumbers in Egypt. (b) More hectares were used to grow cucumbers in Mexico than in Spain. How many more? (c) In Norway, 17 thousand hectares were used to grow cucumbers. Show this information on the bar chart.","answer":"(a) 21 thousand hectares\n(b) 12 thousand more\n(c) Draw Norway bar at 17","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Reading directly from the chart, Egypt = 21 thousand hectares.","(b) Mexico − Spain = 20 − 8 = 12 thousand hectares more.","(c) Draw a bar for Norway reaching up to 17 on the vertical axis."]},{"id":"hg16","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 2F Q24 - Mean (reverse problem)","q":"Kim puts 4 bananas in a bag. The mean weight of the 4 bananas in the bag is 145 grams. Andy puts one more banana into the bag. The mean weight of the 5 bananas in the bag is 142 grams. Work out the weight of the banana that Andy puts into the bag.","answer":"130 grams","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total weight of the original 4 bananas = 4 × 145 = 580 g.","Total weight of all 5 bananas = 5 × 142 = 710 g.","Weight of the banana Andy added = 710 − 580 = 130 g."]},{"id":"hg17","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 1F Q12 - Mean and Probability from a Table","q":"The table shows the number of school lunches each of 30 students had in one week. Number of lunches 0: frequency 2. 1: frequency 5. 2: frequency 11. 3: frequency 7. 4: frequency 4. 5: frequency 1. (a) Work out the mean number of school lunches. (b) The probability that Alex takes a packed lunch to school is 0.79. Work out the probability that Alex does not take a packed lunch to school.","answer":"(a) Mean = 2.3\n(b) P(not packed lunch) = 0.21","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Σ(value × frequency) = 0×2 + 1×5 + 2×11 + 3×7 + 4×4 + 5×1 = 0+5+22+21+16+5 = 69.","Total frequency = 2+5+11+7+4+1 = 30.","Mean = 69 ÷ 30 = 2.3.","(b) Since the only two outcomes are 'packed lunch' and 'not packed lunch', P(not) = 1 − 0.79 = 0.21."]},{"id":"hg18","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 2F Q1 - Draw a Bar Chart","q":"The table shows the distance, in kilometres, each of 6 people travelled to get to a train station: Ted 7, Yui 12, Barney 10, Ichika 5, Wilfred 4, Bella 9. On the grid, draw a bar chart to show this information.","answer":"A bar chart with bars of height 7, 12, 10, 5, 4 and 9 for Ted, Yui, Barney, Ichika, Wilfred and Bella respectively","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Draw one bar for each person on the horizontal axis, with the height of each bar matching their distance in kilometres.","Ted = 7, Yui = 12, Barney = 10, Ichika = 5, Wilfred = 4, Bella = 9 (all bars the same width, drawn to a consistent vertical scale)."]},{"id":"hg19","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 2F Q17 - Median, Mode and Range","q":"Here are eight numbers written in order of size: h, 6, 7, 8, j, 16, k, k, where h, j and k are integers. The median of the eight numbers is 10. The mode of the eight numbers is 18. The range of the eight numbers is 13. Work out the value of h, the value of j and the value of k.","answer":"h = 5, j = 12, k = 18","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Mode = 18: since k is repeated (appears twice), and the mode must be the most frequent value, k = 18.","Median of 8 numbers = average of 4th and 5th values = (8 + j)/2 = 10, so j = 12.","Range = 13: the largest value is k = 18, so the smallest value h = 18 − 13 = 5.","Check order: 5, 6, 7, 8, 12, 16, 18, 18 is correctly ascending."]},{"id":"hg20","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 2F Q28 - Mean (combined groups)","q":"Ali uses a fitness tracker to count the number of steps he walks each day for 7 days. For the first 4 days, his mean number of steps is 11 800. For the next 3 days, his mean number of steps is 13 207. Work out his mean number of steps for the 7 days.","answer":"Mean = 12 403 steps","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total steps for the first 4 days = 4 × 11 800 = 47 200.","Total steps for the next 3 days = 3 × 13 207 = 39 621.","Total steps for all 7 days = 47 200 + 39 621 = 86 821.","Mean for the 7 days = 86 821 ÷ 7 = 12 403 steps."]},{"id":"hg21","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 1F Q2 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart shows the number of international students who studied in each of five countries in 2019: Australia 420, Canada 440, Germany 260, Japan 210, France not yet shown, Spain 120. (a) In which of these five countries did the greatest number of international students study? (b) Write down the number of international students who studied in Australia. (c) The number who studied in Japan was more than the number who studied in Spain. How many more? (d) In 2019, 340 thousand international students studied in France. Draw a bar on the bar chart to show this information.","answer":"(a) Canada\n(b) 420 thousand\n(c) 90 thousand more\n(d) Draw France bar at 340","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Canada's bar is the tallest at 440 thousand, so Canada had the greatest number.","(b) Reading directly from the chart, Australia = 420 thousand.","(c) Japan − Spain = 210 − 120 = 90 thousand more.","(d) Draw a bar for France reaching up to 340 on the vertical axis."]},{"id":"hg22","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 2F Q17 - Modal Class and Estimated Total","q":"The table shows information about the lengths, in minutes, of 50 telephone calls. 0<m≤5: frequency 8. 5<m≤10: frequency 2. 10<m≤15: frequency 6. 15<m≤20: frequency 4. 20<m≤25: frequency 12. 25<m≤30: frequency 18. (a) Write down the modal class. (b) Work out an estimate for the total length, in minutes, of these telephone calls.","answer":"(a) 25 < m ≤ 30\n(b) 945 minutes","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The modal class is the class with the highest frequency, which is 25<m≤30 (frequency 18).","(b) Use midpoints: 2.5, 7.5, 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 2.5×8 + 7.5×2 + 12.5×6 + 17.5×4 + 22.5×12 + 27.5×18","= 20 + 15 + 75 + 70 + 270 + 495 = 945 minutes (this sum directly estimates the total length)."]},{"id":"hg23","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 2F Q24 - Mean (reverse problem)","q":"The mean number of goals scored by a hockey team in 8 matches is 6. The team plays 2 more matches and scores k goals in each match. The mean number of goals scored by the hockey team in the 10 matches is 7. Work out the value of k.","answer":"k = 11","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total goals in the first 8 matches = 8 × 6 = 48.","Total goals in all 10 matches = 10 × 7 = 70.","Goals scored in the 2 extra matches = 70 − 48 = 22, so each of the 2 matches scored 22 ÷ 2 = 11 goals.","k = 11."]},{"id":"hg24","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1F Q2 - Reading a Table","q":"Melanie finds this information about the number of people, in millions, who speak each of five languages as their first language: Spanish 480, Greek 13, Mandarin Chinese 918, Tamil 75, Japanese 128. (a) Which of these languages is the first language of the greatest number of people? (b) More people speak Japanese as their first language than speak Greek as their first language. How many more? (c) The number of people who speak Tamil as their first language is 1/4 of the number who speak Bengali as their first language. Work out the number of people who speak Bengali as their first language. (d) It is estimated that 861 700 people can speak Welsh. Write 861 700 in words.","answer":"(a) Mandarin Chinese\n(b) 115 million more\n(c) 300 million\n(d) eight hundred and sixty-one thousand, seven hundred","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Mandarin Chinese has the largest number (918 million).","(b) Japanese − Greek = 128 − 13 = 115 million more.","(c) Tamil = 75 million = 1/4 of Bengali, so Bengali = 75 × 4 = 300 million.","(d) 861 700 written in words is 'eight hundred and sixty-one thousand, seven hundred'."]},{"id":"hg25","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1F Q17 - Estimated Total from a Table","q":"The table gives information about the number of minutes that Abby spent walking each day in September. 0<M≤30: frequency 5. 30<M≤60: frequency 6. 60<M≤90: frequency 8. 90<M≤120: frequency 9. 120<M≤150: frequency 2. Work out an estimate for the total number of minutes that Abby spent walking in September.","answer":"2160 minutes","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Use midpoints: 15, 45, 75, 105, 135.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 15×5 + 45×6 + 75×8 + 105×9 + 135×2","= 75 + 270 + 600 + 945 + 270 = 2160 minutes."]},{"id":"hg26","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 2F Q3 - Pictogram","q":"Peter owns boarding kennels for dogs. The pictogram shows the number of dogs at the kennels each day from Monday to Thursday last week, where a full oval represents 8 dogs. Monday: 3 full + 1 half. Tuesday: 1 full + 1 quarter. Wednesday: 1 full + 1 three-quarter. Thursday: 2 full + 1 half. (a) How many dogs were at the kennels on Monday? (b) 18 dogs were at the kennels on Friday. Show this information on the pictogram. (c) Peter gave each dog 2 biscuits each day. Work out the total number of biscuits that Peter gave the dogs during the five days from Monday to Friday last week.","answer":"(a) 28 dogs\n(b) 2 full ovals + 1 quarter oval\n(c) 180 biscuits","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Monday = 3 full + 1 half = 3×8 + 4 = 28 dogs.","Tuesday = 1 full + 1 quarter = 8 + 2 = 10. Wednesday = 1 full + 3/4 = 8 + 6 = 14. Thursday = 2 full + 1 half = 16 + 4 = 20.","(b) 18 dogs ÷ 8 = 2.25 ovals, so draw 2 full ovals and a 1/4 oval.","(c) Total dogs over the 5 days = 28 + 10 + 14 + 20 + 18 = 90. Each dog gets 2 biscuits a day, so total biscuits = 90 × 2 = 180."]},{"id":"hg27","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 2F Q11 - Pie Chart Angles and Frequencies","q":"Trisha asked 90 people to name their favourite type of cheese to put on a pizza. 55 of the 90 people said that mozzarella was their favourite. (a) Work out the size of the angle on the pie chart for the sector representing mozzarella. Haashir asked a different group of people about their favourite ice cream flavour and drew a pie chart: mint 35°, chocolate 75°, vanilla 195°, strawberry 55°. 39 of the people Haashir asked said that vanilla was their favourite. (b) Work out how many of the people Haashir asked said that chocolate was their favourite.","answer":"(a) 220°\n(b) 15 people","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Mozzarella angle = (55/90) × 360° = 220°.","(b) Vanilla: 39 people = 195°, so 1 person corresponds to 195/39 = 5°, meaning the total number of people = 360 ÷ 5 = 72.","Chocolate = 75° ÷ 5° per person = 15 people."]},{"id":"hg28","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 2F Q26 - Mean (combined groups, reverse)","q":"Team A and Team B take part in a quiz league. After 11 rounds, Team A has a mean score per round of 17. After 9 rounds, Team B has a mean score per round of 18. Both teams take part in a further round. After this round, both teams have a mean score per round of 18.5. In the further round, Team A scored more points than Team B. How many more?","answer":"12 more points","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Team A's total after 11 rounds = 11 × 17 = 187. After 12 rounds, total = 12 × 18.5 = 222, so Team A scored 222 − 187 = 35 points in the further round.","Team B's total after 9 rounds = 9 × 18 = 162. After 10 rounds, total = 10 × 18.5 = 185, so Team B scored 185 − 162 = 23 points in the further round.","Team A scored 35 − 23 = 12 more points than Team B in the further round."]},{"id":"hg29","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 1F Q16 - Estimated Mean","q":"80 students entered a dancing competition. The table gives information about the length of time, in minutes, for which each student spent dancing. 0<m≤12: frequency 11. 12<m≤24: frequency 25. 24<m≤36: frequency 23. 36<m≤48: frequency 15. 48<m≤60: frequency 6. Work out an estimate for the mean length of time the students spent dancing.","answer":"Mean = 27 minutes","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Use midpoints: 6, 18, 30, 42, 54.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 6×11 + 18×25 + 30×23 + 42×15 + 54×6","= 66 + 450 + 690 + 630 + 324 = 2160.","Total frequency = 11+25+23+15+6 = 80.","Estimated mean = 2160 ÷ 80 = 27 minutes."]},{"id":"hg30","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2F Q2 - Pictogram","q":"The pictogram shows information about the total weight of potatoes grown last year in each of five countries, where a full oval represents 4 million tonnes. Bangladesh: 2 full + 1 half. USA: 5 full. Germany: 2 full + 1 three-quarter. Poland: 2 full + 1 quarter. France: 1 full + 1 three-quarter. The Netherlands row is blank. The pictogram shows one country where the total weight of potatoes grown last year was 20 million tonnes. (a) Which country? Last year, the weight of potatoes grown in The Netherlands was 6 million tonnes. (b) Show this information on the pictogram. (c) Work out the total weight of potatoes grown in Germany and in France last year.","answer":"(a) USA\n(b) 1 full oval + 1 half oval\n(c) 18 million tonnes","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Bangladesh = 2 full + 1 half = 2×4+2 = 10. USA = 5×4 = 20. Germany = 2 full + 3/4 = 8+3 = 11. Poland = 2 full + 1/4 = 8+1 = 9. France = 1 full + 3/4 = 4+3 = 7.","(a) 20 million tonnes matches USA.","(b) The Netherlands = 6 ÷ 4 = 1.5 ovals, so draw 1 full oval and 1 half oval.","(c) Germany + France = 11 + 7 = 18 million tonnes."]},{"id":"hg31","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 1F Q12 - Mean and Probability","q":"The table gives information about the number of gold stars won by each of 25 students in class 7T last week. Number of gold stars 0: frequency 6. 1: frequency 5. 2: frequency 4. 3: frequency 7. 4: frequency 3. (a) Work out the mean number of gold stars won. A student in class 8R is to be chosen at random. The probability that this student won at least one gold star last week is 0.39. (b) Work out the probability that this student did not win at least one gold star last week.","answer":"(a) Mean = 1.84\n(b) P(not at least one) = 0.61","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Σ(value × frequency) = 0×6 + 1×5 + 2×4 + 3×7 + 4×3 = 0+5+8+21+12 = 46.","Total frequency = 6+5+4+7+3 = 25.","Mean = 46 ÷ 25 = 1.84.","(b) P(not at least one) = 1 − 0.39 = 0.61."]},{"id":"hg32","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2FR Q2 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart gives information about the total number, in millions, of cars produced in 2017 and 2018 for each of five countries: Japan 19, India 10, Spain 6, USA 22, France 5, Mexico not yet shown. The total number of cars produced in 2017 and 2018 in Mexico was 8 million. (a) Draw a bar on the bar chart to show this information. (b) Which of these six countries produced the greatest total number of cars? (c) Which country produced half as many cars as India? (d) Work out the difference between the total number of cars produced in Japan and the total number of cars produced in Spain.","answer":"(a) Draw Mexico bar at 8\n(b) USA\n(c) France\n(d) 13 million","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Draw a bar for Mexico reaching up to 8 on the vertical axis.","(b) USA has the tallest bar at 22 million, so USA produced the greatest total number.","(c) Half of India's 10 million = 5 million, which matches France.","(d) Japan − Spain = 19 − 6 = 13 million."]},{"id":"hg33","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2FR Q16 - Mode and Mean","q":"The table shows information about the number of mobile phones owned by each of 40 families. Number of mobile phones 0: frequency 1. 1: frequency 5. 2: frequency 12. 3: frequency 9. 4: frequency 11. 5: frequency 2. For the information in the table, (a) write down the mode, (b) work out the mean.","answer":"(a) Mode = 2\n(b) Mean = 2.75","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The mode is the value with the highest frequency, which is 2 (frequency 12).","(b) Σ(value × frequency) = 0×1 + 1×5 + 2×12 + 3×9 + 4×11 + 5×2 = 0+5+24+27+44+10 = 110.","Total frequency = 1+5+12+9+11+2 = 40.","Mean = 110 ÷ 40 = 2.75."]},{"id":"hg34","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2FR Q20 - Median and Range","q":"Alberto, Bill, Candela and Diana are four friends. Alberto's height is 158 cm. Bill's height is 175 cm. Candela's height is greater than Diana's height. The median height of these four friends is 160 cm. The range of the heights of these four friends is 21 cm. Work out Candela's height and Diana's height.","answer":"Candela = 162 cm, Diana = 154 cm","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Arrange the four heights in order. Try Diana as the minimum: with Bill (175 cm) as the maximum, range = 175 − Diana = 21, so Diana = 154 cm.","The median of 4 values is the average of the 2nd and 3rd values: (Alberto + Candela)/2 = (158 + Candela)/2 = 160, so Candela = 162 cm.","Check the order: Diana (154), Alberto (158), Candela (162), Bill (175) — ascending, with Candela > Diana as required.","Range = 175 − 154 = 21 ✓. Median = (158+162)/2 = 160 ✓."]},{"id":"hg35","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2F Q4 - Bar Chart","q":"The bar chart shows the weight, in millions of tonnes, of the potatoes produced by each of four countries in 2016: Country A 20, Country B 11, Country C 31, Country D 9, Country E not yet shown. In 2016, one of these four countries produced 11 million tonnes of potatoes. (a) Which country? In 2016, Country E produced 7 million tonnes of potatoes. (b) Draw a bar on the bar chart to show this information. In 2016, the weight of potatoes produced by Country C was greater than the weight produced by Country A. (c) How many million tonnes greater?","answer":"(a) Country B\n(b) Draw Country E bar at 7\n(c) 11 million tonnes greater","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Country B's bar shows 11 million tonnes.","(b) Draw a bar for Country E reaching up to 7 on the vertical axis.","(c) Country C − Country A = 31 − 20 = 11 million tonnes."]},{"id":"hg36","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2F Q17 - Mode, Median and Range","q":"Here are some integers where a < b < c < d: a, b, c, d, d, d. The mode of the integers is 9. The median of the integers is 8. The range of the integers is 4. Work out the value of a, the value of b, the value of c and the value of d.","answer":"a = 5, b = 6, c = 7, d = 9","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Mode = 9: since d is repeated 3 times (the most of any value), d = 9.","Median of 6 numbers = average of 3rd and 4th values = (c + d)/2 = (c+9)/2 = 8, so c = 7.","Range = 4: d − a = 9 − a = 4, so a = 5.","Since a < b < c, i.e. 5 < b < 7, and b must be an integer, b = 6."]},{"id":"hg37","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2F Q28 - Mean (combined groups)","q":"Larry is a delivery man. He has 7 parcels to deliver. The mean weight of the 7 parcels is 2.7 kg. Larry delivers 3 of the parcels. Each of these 3 parcels has a weight of W kg. The mean weight of the other 4 parcels is 3.3 kg. Work out the value of W.","answer":"W = 1.9 kg","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total weight of all 7 parcels = 7 × 2.7 = 18.9 kg.","Total weight of the other 4 parcels = 4 × 3.3 = 13.2 kg.","Total weight of the 3 parcels Larry delivers = 18.9 − 13.2 = 5.7 kg.","W = 5.7 ÷ 3 = 1.9 kg."]},{"id":"hg38","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 1F Q2 - Pictogram","q":"The pictogram shows information about the number of text messages Colin sent on each of four days last week, where a full rectangle (2 cells) represents 8 text messages. Monday: 4 full. Tuesday: 3 full. Wednesday: 2 full + 1 half. Thursday: 2 full + 1 three-quarter. (a) How many text messages did Colin send on Tuesday? (b) Work out the total number of text messages that Colin sent on the four days from Monday to Thursday last week. (c) On Friday, Colin sent 26 text messages. Show this information on the pictogram.","answer":"(a) 24 messages\n(b) 102 messages\n(c) 3 full rectangles + 1 quarter rectangle","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Monday = 4 × 8 = 32. Tuesday = 3 × 8 = 24. Wednesday = 2 full + 1 half = 16+4 = 20. Thursday = 2 full + 3/4 = 16+6 = 26.","(a) Tuesday = 24 text messages.","(b) Total = 32 + 24 + 20 + 26 = 102 text messages.","(c) Friday = 26 ÷ 8 = 3.25, so draw 3 full rectangles and a 1/4 rectangle."]},{"id":"hg39","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 2FR Q14 - Pie Chart with Votes","q":"Anjali, Ravina and Sandeep were the three candidates in an election. Heidi draws a pie chart for the number of votes received by each of the three candidates. The angle in the pie chart for the number of votes received by Anjali is 90°. The angle in the pie chart for the number of votes received by Ravina is 160°. Ravina received 400 votes. Work out the number of votes Sandeep received.","answer":"275 votes","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Ravina: 400 votes = 160°, so 1 vote corresponds to 160/400 = 0.4°, meaning the total number of votes = 360 ÷ 0.4 = 900.","Sandeep's angle = 360 − 90 − 160 = 110°.","Sandeep's votes = (110/360) × 900 = 275."]},{"id":"hg40","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 2FR Q25 - Weighted Mean","q":"A scientist is investigating the weight of 50 tigers. There are 22 Siberian tigers with a mean weight of 260 kg, and 28 Bengal tigers. The mean weight of all 50 tigers is 218 kg. Work out the mean weight of the Bengal tigers.","answer":"Mean weight of Bengal tigers = 185 kg","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total weight of all 50 tigers = 50 × 218 = 10 900 kg.","Total weight of the Siberian tigers = 22 × 260 = 5720 kg.","Total weight of the Bengal tigers = 10 900 − 5720 = 5180 kg.","Mean weight of Bengal tigers = 5180 ÷ 28 = 185 kg."]},{"id":"hg41","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 2F Q24 - Mean (unknown frequency)","q":"The frequency table gives information about the number of points scored by a player. Number of points 0: frequency 13. 1: frequency 17. 2: frequency 8. 3: frequency x. 4: frequency 11. The mean number of points scored is 2. Work out the value of x.","answer":"x = 21","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total frequency = 13+17+8+x+11 = 49+x.","Σ(value × frequency) = 0×13 + 1×17 + 2×8 + 3x + 4×11 = 17+16+3x+44 = 77+3x.","Mean = (77+3x)/(49+x) = 2.","77 + 3x = 2(49+x) = 98 + 2x, so x = 21."]},{"id":"hg42","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 1F Q10 - Two-Way Table and Probability","q":"The two-way table shows some information about the city a group of students and teachers would most like to visit from Berlin, Paris and Rome. Students: Berlin 29, Paris 25, Rome and total unknown. Teachers: Berlin and Paris unknown, Rome 19, total 52. Column totals: Berlin 42, Paris unknown, Rome unknown, grand total 140. (a) Complete the two-way table. (b) One of the teachers is chosen at random. Find the probability that this teacher would most like to visit Rome.","answer":"(a) Students: Berlin 29, Paris 25, Rome 34, total 88. Teachers: Berlin 13, Paris 20, Rome 19, total 52. Totals: Berlin 42, Paris 45, Rome 53, grand total 140\n(b) P = 19/52","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Teachers Berlin = Berlin total (42) − students Berlin (29) = 13.","Teachers total is 52, so teachers Paris = 52 − 13 − 19 = 20.","Students total = grand total (140) − teachers total (52) = 88, so Rome students = 88 − 29 − 25 = 34.","Paris total = 20+25 = 45. Rome total = 34+19 = 53. Check: 42+45+53 = 140. ✓","(b) P(Rome | teacher) = 19/52."]},{"id":"hg43","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 2F Q20 - Modal Class and Estimated Mean","q":"The table shows information about the number of hours each of 40 candles burned. 15<h≤19: frequency 2. 19<h≤23: frequency 5. 23<h≤27: frequency 12. 27<h≤31: frequency 15. 31<h≤35: frequency 6. (a) Write down the modal class. (b) Work out an estimate for the mean number of hours.","answer":"(a) 27 < h ≤ 31\n(b) Mean = 26.8 hours","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The modal class has the highest frequency: 27<h≤31 (frequency 15).","(b) Use midpoints: 17, 21, 25, 29, 33.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 17×2 + 21×5 + 25×12 + 29×15 + 33×6 = 34+105+300+435+198 = 1072.","Total frequency = 2+5+12+15+6 = 40.","Estimated mean = 1072 ÷ 40 = 26.8 hours."]},{"id":"hg44","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 2F Q11 - Pie Chart, Frequencies and Probability","q":"The pie chart gives information about the types of vehicles parked in a car park: motorcycle 45°, car 144°, bus unknown, other 72°. (a) Which type of vehicle is the mode? 5 of the vehicles are motorcycles. (b) Work out the number of buses. Kim selects at random one of the vehicles in the car park. (c) What is the probability that she selects a motorcycle?","answer":"(a) Car\n(b) 11 buses\n(c) P(motorcycle) = 1/8","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The mode is the type with the largest sector, which is car (144°).","(b) Bus angle = 360 − 45 − 144 − 72 = 99°. Motorcycles: 5 vehicles = 45°, so 1 vehicle = 9°, meaning the total number of vehicles = 360 ÷ 9 = 40.","Buses = 99° ÷ 9° per vehicle = 11 buses.","(c) P(motorcycle) = 5/40 = 1/8."]},{"id":"hg45","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 1F Q18 - Modal Class and Estimated Mean","q":"The table gives information about the distances 100 adults travel to work. 0<d≤5: frequency 26. 5<d≤10: frequency 40. 10<d≤15: frequency 16. 15<d≤20: frequency 10. 20<d≤25: frequency 8. (a) Write down the modal class. (b) Work out an estimate for the mean distance.","answer":"(a) 5 < d ≤ 10\n(b) Mean = 9.2 km","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The modal class has the highest frequency: 5<d≤10 (frequency 40).","(b) Use midpoints: 2.5, 7.5, 12.5, 17.5, 22.5.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 2.5×26 + 7.5×40 + 12.5×16 + 17.5×10 + 22.5×8 = 65+300+200+175+180 = 920.","Total frequency = 26+40+16+10+8 = 100.","Estimated mean = 920 ÷ 100 = 9.2 km."]},{"id":"hg46","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 2F Q2 - Tally, Frequency Table and Bar Chart","q":"Zia asks 20 adults to name their favourite African animal. Her results: elephant, rhinoceros, rhinoceros, lion, elephant, lion, zebra, elephant, rhinoceros, lion, buffalo, elephant, buffalo, buffalo, elephant, rhinoceros, lion, rhinoceros, elephant, zebra. (a) Complete the frequency table to show this information. (b) Complete the bar chart for the information in your table.","answer":"(a) elephant 6, lion 4, buffalo 3, rhinoceros 5, zebra 2\n(b) Bar chart with bars of these heights for each animal","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Count each animal from the list: elephant appears 6 times, lion appears 4 times, buffalo appears 3 times, rhinoceros appears 5 times, zebra appears 2 times.","Check: 6+4+3+5+2 = 20, matching the 20 adults asked.","(b) Draw a bar chart with the heights of the bars equal to the frequencies found in part (a)."]},{"id":"hg47","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 1F Q18 - Modal Class and Estimated Mean","q":"The table shows some information about the hourly rates of pay of 60 workers. 10<p≤15: frequency 18. 15<p≤20: frequency 16. 20<p≤25: frequency 14. 25<p≤30: frequency 8. 30<p≤35: frequency 4. (a) Write down the modal class. (b) Work out an estimate for the mean hourly rate of pay of the 60 workers.","answer":"(a) 10 < p ≤ 15\n(b) Mean = $19.50","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The modal class has the highest frequency: 10<p≤15 (frequency 18).","(b) Use midpoints: 12.5, 17.5, 22.5, 27.5, 32.5.","Σ(midpoint × frequency) = 12.5×18 + 17.5×16 + 22.5×14 + 27.5×8 + 32.5×4 = 225+280+315+220+130 = 1170.","Total frequency = 18+16+14+8+4 = 60.","Estimated mean = 1170 ÷ 60 = $19.50."]},{"id":"hg48","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 2F Q26 - Pie Chart and Algebraic Table","q":"All the students in year 10 and all the students in year 11 named their favourite science subject from Biology, Chemistry and Physics. The pie chart for year 10 shows Biology 126°, Chemistry 102°, Physics 132°. The table for year 11 shows Biology = 3x+6, Chemistry = 5x+8, Physics = 7x−9. There are 300 students in year 10. There are 320 students in year 11. More students in year 10 than in year 11 said Biology was their favourite science subject. How many more?","answer":"36 more students","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Year 10 Biology = (126/360) × 300 = 105 students.","Year 11 total: (3x+6) + (5x+8) + (7x−9) = 15x+5 = 320, so x = 21.","Year 11 Biology = 3(21)+6 = 69 students.","Difference = 105 − 69 = 36 more students in year 10."]},{"id":"hg49","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 1F Q10 - Mode, Mean and Probability","q":"The table gives information about the number of rewards gained by each of 50 students last term. Number of rewards 0: frequency 3. 1: frequency 9. 2: frequency 15. 3: frequency 18. 4: frequency 4. 5: frequency 1. (a) Write down the mode of the number of rewards. (b) Work out the mean number of rewards. One of these students is chosen at random. (c) Find the probability that this student gained more than 2 rewards.","answer":"(a) Mode = 3\n(b) Mean = 2.28\n(c) P(more than 2) = 23/50","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The mode is the value with the highest frequency, which is 3 (frequency 18).","(b) Σ(value × frequency) = 0×3+1×9+2×15+3×18+4×4+5×1 = 0+9+30+54+16+5 = 114.","Total frequency = 3+9+15+18+4+1 = 50. Mean = 114 ÷ 50 = 2.28.","(c) 'More than 2' means 3, 4 or 5 rewards: frequency = 18+4+1 = 23. P = 23/50."]},{"id":"hg50","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 2F Q2 - Pictogram and Ratio","q":"The pictogram gives information about the number of parcels a company posted on each of four days last week, where a full rectangle (2 cells) represents 16 parcels. Monday: 3 full + 1 half. Tuesday: 3 full + 1 quarter. Wednesday: 4 full + 1 three-quarter. Thursday: 3 full. (a) How many parcels were posted on Tuesday? 24 parcels were posted on Friday. (b) Show this information on the pictogram. More parcels were posted on Wednesday than on Monday. (c) How many more? (d) Find the ratio of the number of parcels posted on Monday to the number of parcels posted on Thursday, in its simplest form.","answer":"(a) 52 parcels\n(b) 1 full rectangle + 1 half rectangle\n(c) 20 more\n(d) 7 : 6","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Monday = 3 full + 1 half = 3×16+8 = 56. Tuesday = 3 full + 1 quarter = 48+4 = 52. Wednesday = 4 full + 3/4 = 64+12 = 76. Thursday = 3×16 = 48.","(a) Tuesday = 52 parcels.","(b) Friday = 24 ÷ 16 = 1.5 rectangles, so draw 1 full rectangle and 1 half rectangle.","(c) Wednesday − Monday = 76 − 56 = 20 more parcels.","(d) Monday : Thursday = 56 : 48 = 7 : 6 (dividing both by 8)."]},{"id":"hg51","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1F Q3 - Tally, Frequency Table and Bar Chart","q":"Ivor asks 20 children in his class to name their favourite fruit. His results: apple, orange, grape, peach, grape, banana, apple, orange, grape, peach, apple, apple, banana, peach, orange, grape, orange, apple, orange, orange. (a) Complete the frequency table to show this information. (b) Complete the bar chart for the information in your table.","answer":"(a) apple 5, banana 2, grape 4, peach 3, orange 6\n(b) Bar chart with bars of these heights for each fruit","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Count each fruit from the list: apple appears 5 times, banana 2 times, grape 4 times, peach 3 times, orange 6 times.","Check: 5+2+4+3+6 = 20, matching the 20 children asked.","(b) Draw a bar chart with the heights of the bars equal to the frequencies found in part (a)."]},{"id":"hg52","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1F Q12 - Range, Median and Mean (find x)","q":"Pam plays netball for her school team. Here are the numbers of goals she scored in the last 8 games, written in order of size: 1, 1, 2, 2, 3, 6, x, 14. (a) Find the range of the number of goals Pam scored. (b) Find the median number of goals Pam scored. The mean number of goals Pam scored in the 8 games is 5. (c) Work out the value of x.","answer":"(a) Range = 13\n(b) Median = 2.5\n(c) x = 11","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Range = largest − smallest = 14 − 1 = 13.","(b) Median of 8 values = average of 4th and 5th values = (2+3)/2 = 2.5.","(c) Mean = 5, so total = 8 × 5 = 40. Sum of known values = 1+1+2+2+3+6+14 = 29.","x = 40 − 29 = 11 (and 6 ≤ 11 ≤ 14, so it fits correctly in the ordered list)."]},{"id":"hg53","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2F Q9 - Two-Way Table, Percentage and Pie Chart Angle","q":"There are 120 cyclists in a cycling club. There are 67 professional cyclists and the rest are amateur cyclists. The two-way table shows: Professional road bike 26, total 67; Amateur mountain bike 32; column totals road bike 39, mountain bike 54, grand total 120. (a) Complete the table. (b) Work out the percentage of the cyclists who answered Mountain bike. Jacob is going to draw a pie chart for the age groups of the 120 cyclists. There are 41 people in the 'over 60' age group. (c) Work out the size of the angle for the sector representing the 'over 60' age group.","answer":"(a) Professional: 26, 22, 19, total 67. Amateur: 13, 32, 8, total 53. Totals: 39, 54, 27, grand total 120\n(b) 45%\n(c) 123°","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Amateur road bike = 39 − 26 = 13. Amateur total = 120 − 67 = 53.","Professional mountain bike = 54 − 32 = 22. Professional hybrid = 67 − 26 − 22 = 19.","Amateur hybrid = 53 − 13 − 32 = 8. Hybrid total = 19+8 = 27. Check: 39+54+27 = 120. ✓","(b) Percentage mountain bike = (54/120) × 100 = 45%.","(c) Pie chart angle for 'over 60' = (41/120) × 360° = 123°."]},{"id":"hg54","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2F Q10 - Mode, Median and Total from a Table","q":"The frequency table shows information about the number of cookies made by each of the 21 people in a cookery class. Number of cookies 10: frequency 1. 11: frequency 7. 12: frequency 2. 13: frequency 5. 14: frequency 4. 15: frequency 2. (a) Write down the mode of the number of cookies made. (b) Find the median number of cookies made. (c) Find the total number of cookies made by the 21 people in the cookery class.","answer":"(a) Mode = 11\n(b) Median = 13\n(c) Total = 262 cookies","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The mode is the value with the highest frequency, which is 11 (frequency 7).","(b) There are 21 values, so the median is the 11th value. Cumulative totals: up to 10 → 1, up to 11 → 8, up to 12 → 10, up to 13 → 15.","The 11th value falls in the '13' group, so the median is 13.","(c) Total = 10×1+11×7+12×2+13×5+14×4+15×2 = 10+77+24+65+56+30 = 262 cookies."]},{"id":"hg55","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 1F Q6 - Two-Way Table and Conditional Probability","q":"Bohai works in a shop that sells mobile phones. Last week he sold one mobile phone to each of 300 customers. The incomplete two-way table shows: type A, 32GB 75, 128GB 83, total 195; type B, 64GB 29; column totals 32GB 127, grand total 300. (a) Complete the two-way table. Bohai selects at random one of these 300 customers. (b) Write down the probability that this customer bought a type B, 64GB mobile phone. Bohai now selects at random one of the customers who bought a type A phone last week. (c) Write down the probability that this customer bought a 128GB mobile phone.","answer":"(a) Type A: 75, 37, 83, total 195. Type B: 52, 29, 24, total 105. Totals: 127, 66, 107, grand total 300\n(b) P = 29/300\n(c) P = 83/195","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Type A 64GB = 195 − 75 − 83 = 37. Type B 32GB = 127 − 75 = 52.","64GB total = 37+29 = 66. Type B total = 300 − 195 = 105. Type B 128GB = 105 − 52 − 29 = 24.","128GB total = 83+24 = 107. Check: 127+66+107 = 300. ✓","(b) P(type B, 64GB) = 29/300.","(c) P(128GB | type A) = 83/195."]},{"id":"hg56","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 1F Q20 - Ordered List and Mean (reverse)","q":"Jenny has six cards. Each card has a whole number written on it so that the smallest number is 5, the largest number is 24, the median of the six numbers is 14, and the mode of the six numbers is 8. Jenny arranges her cards so that the numbers are in order of size: 5, [ ], [ ], [ ], [ ], 24. (a) For the remaining four cards, write a number that could be on each card. A basketball team plays 6 games. After playing 5 games, the team has a mean score of 21 points per game. After playing 6 games, the team has a mean score of 23 points per game. (b) Work out the number of points the team scored in its 6th game.","answer":"(a) One valid set: 8, 8, 20, 22 (full list 5, 8, 8, 20, 22, 24)\n(b) 33 points","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Median of 6 numbers = average of 3rd and 4th values = 14, so these two values sum to 28. Mode = 8, so 8 must appear at least twice.","A valid solution: 3rd and 4th values = 8 and 20 (summing to 28), with another 8 in the 2nd position so that 8 is repeated (and is therefore the mode). A value between 20 and 24 (e.g. 22) fills the 5th position.","Full ordered list: 5, 8, 8, 20, 22, 24. Check: smallest 5 ✓, largest 24 ✓, median (8+20)/2 = 14 ✓, mode 8 ✓.","(b) Total after 5 games = 5 × 21 = 105. Total after 6 games = 6 × 23 = 138.","6th game score = 138 − 105 = 33 points."]},{"id":"hg57","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 1H Q16 - Interquartile Range","q":"Ivan asked 15 people how many books they read last year. Here are his results, in order: 1, 1, 3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 12, 15, 25, 30, 37, 50. Work out the interquartile range of the number of books read.","answer":"IQR = 21","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With n = 15 values, the lower quartile is the (n+1)/4 = 4th value, and the upper quartile is the 3(n+1)/4 = 12th value.","4th value = 4 (lower quartile). 12th value = 25 (upper quartile).","Interquartile range = 25 − 4 = 21."]},{"id":"hg58","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 2H Q13 - Interquartile Range","q":"Robert asked 11 people how many meetings they attended last week. Here are the results in numerical order: 1, 2, 4, 6, 6, 8, 11, 12, 13, 14, 17. Find the interquartile range of the number of meetings.","answer":"IQR = 9","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With n = 11 values, the lower quartile is the (n+1)/4 = 3rd value, and the upper quartile is the 3(n+1)/4 = 9th value.","3rd value = 4 (lower quartile). 9th value = 13 (upper quartile).","Interquartile range = 13 − 4 = 9."]},{"id":"hg59","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1H Q10 - Interquartile Range","q":"Here are the test marks of 15 students: 7, 10, 14, 15, 16, 17, 18, 19, 20, 20, 23, 25, 30, 39, 40. Find the interquartile range of these marks.","answer":"IQR = 10","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With n = 15 values, the lower quartile is the (n+1)/4 = 4th value, and the upper quartile is the 3(n+1)/4 = 12th value.","4th value = 15 (lower quartile). 12th value = 25 (upper quartile).","Interquartile range = 25 − 15 = 10."]},{"id":"hg60","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 2H Q13 - Combined Mean from Partial Data","q":"Leo collects apples from three trees. The incomplete table gives information about the apples he collects from these trees. Tree A: 12 apples, mean weight 150 g. Tree B: 20 apples, total weight 2700 g. Tree C: mean weight 145 g, total weight 2610 g. Work out the mean weight of all the apples Leo collects from these three trees.","answer":"Mean = 142.2 g","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Tree A total weight = 12 × 150 = 1800 g.","Tree B mean weight = 2700 ÷ 20 = 135 g (not needed further, but total weight is already given as 2700 g).","Tree C number of apples = 2610 ÷ 145 = 18 apples.","Total apples = 12 + 20 + 18 = 50. Total weight = 1800 + 2700 + 2610 = 7110 g.","Overall mean = 7110 ÷ 50 = 142.2 g."]},{"id":"hg61","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 2H Q14 - Cumulative Frequency: Median and IQR","q":"The cumulative frequency graph gives information about the weights, in kg, of 60 crates. (a) Use the graph to find an estimate for the median weight. (b) Use the graph to find an estimate for the interquartile range.","answer":"(a) Median ≈ 117 kg\n(b) IQR ≈ 17 kg","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The median corresponds to a cumulative frequency of n/2 = 30. Reading across from 30 on the vertical axis to the curve, then down to the horizontal axis, gives a median weight of approximately 117 kg.","(b) The lower quartile corresponds to cumulative frequency n/4 = 15, giving approximately 111 kg.","The upper quartile corresponds to cumulative frequency 3n/4 = 45, giving approximately 128 kg.","Interquartile range ≈ 128 − 111 = 17 kg.","(Readings from a cumulative frequency graph are estimates — answers within a small range of these values are acceptable.)"]},{"id":"hg62","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 2H Q21 - Histogram: Estimating a Frequency","q":"The histogram shows information about the times, in minutes, that some trains arrived late at a station one day, with class boundaries at 0, 10, 15, 30, 40 and 60 minutes. 20 of these trains arrived between 10 minutes late and 15 minutes late. No trains arrived more than 60 minutes late. Work out an estimate for the number of these trains that arrived at least 25 minutes late.","answer":"Approximately 42 trains","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The bar for 10–15 minutes has width 5 and represents 20 trains, so its frequency density = 20 ÷ 5 = 4; this calibrates the vertical scale of the histogram.","Reading the other bars relative to this scale: the 15–30 minute bar has frequency density ≈3.5, the 30–40 minute bar ≈1, and the 40–60 minute bar ≈0.7.","'At least 25 minutes late' covers part of the 15–30 bar (from 25 to 30, width 5) plus all of the 30–40 and 40–60 bars.","Estimate: (3.5×5) + (1×10) + (0.7×20) = 17.5 + 10 + 14 = 41.5 ≈ 42 trains."]},{"id":"hg63","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 1H Q17 - Histogram: Proportion","q":"The histogram shows information about the times, in minutes, that some people were in a shop, with class boundaries at 0, 10, 15, 30, 50 and 60 minutes and frequency densities 2.5, 4, 3.6, 0.5 and 1.7 respectively. Work out an estimate for the proportion of these people who were in the shop for more than 40 minutes.","answer":"Proportion ≈ 11/63 ≈ 0.175","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Frequency = frequency density × class width for each bar: [0,10):2.5×10=25, [10,15):4×5=20, [15,30):3.6×15=54, [30,50):0.5×20=10, [50,60):1.7×10=17.","Total number of people = 25+20+54+10+17 = 126.","'More than 40 minutes' covers part of the [30,50) bar (from 40 to 50, width 10) plus all of [50,60).","Frequency(>40) = (0.5×10) + 17 = 5 + 17 = 22.","Proportion = 22/126 = 11/63 ≈ 0.175."]},{"id":"hg64","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 1H Q18 - Histogram: Estimating a Fraction","q":"The histogram shows information about the times some students took to complete a puzzle, with class boundaries at 0, 10, 25, 30, 60 and 65 minutes and frequency densities 1.5, 0.8, 4, 2.5 and 4.6 respectively. Work out an estimate for the fraction of these students who took between 20 minutes and 60 minutes to complete the puzzle.","answer":"Fraction = 99/145","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Frequency = frequency density × class width: [0,10):1.5×10=15, [10,25):0.8×15=12, [25,30):4×5=20, [30,60):2.5×30=75, [60,65):4.6×5=23.","Total number of students = 15+12+20+75+23 = 145.","'Between 20 and 60 minutes' covers part of [10,25) (from 20 to 25, width 5) plus all of [25,30) and [30,60).","Frequency(20 to 60) = (0.8×5) + 20 + 75 = 4 + 20 + 75 = 99.","Fraction = 99/145 (this does not simplify further, since 145 = 5×29 and 99 = 9×11 share no common factor)."]},{"id":"hg65","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 1H Q22 - Completing a Histogram","q":"The incomplete histogram shows some information about the distances, in kilometres, that 100 adults ran last week, with bars already drawn for 15–20 km, 20–35 km and 35–55 km. All of the adults ran at least 5 kilometres. None of the adults ran more than 55 kilometres. 14 adults ran between 15 kilometres and 20 kilometres. Complete the histogram (find the missing bar for 5–15 km).","answer":"The missing bar (5–15 km) has frequency 18, drawn at frequency density 1.8","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The 15–20 km bar represents 14 adults over a width of 5, so its frequency density = 14 ÷ 5 = 2.8; this calibrates the histogram's vertical scale.","Reading the drawn bars using this scale: 20–35 km has frequency density 4 (frequency = 4×15 = 60), and 35–55 km has frequency density 0.4 (frequency = 0.4×20 = 8).","Since all 100 adults ran between 5 km and 55 km, the frequencies of all classes must sum to 100.","Missing frequency (5–15 km) = 100 − 14 − 60 − 8 = 18.","Frequency density for the missing bar = 18 ÷ 10 = 1.8, so draw a bar from 5 to 15 km reaching a height of 1.8."]},{"id":"hg66","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 1H Q13 - Cumulative Frequency: Median and Percentage","q":"The cumulative frequency graph gives information about the times, in seconds, that 80 adults took to log in to an online bank. (a) Find an estimate for the median time. (b) Work out the percentage of these adults that took longer than 50 seconds to log in. Show your working clearly.","answer":"(a) Median ≈ 26 seconds\n(b) ≈ 15%","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 40. Reading across from 40 to the curve and down to the time axis gives a median of approximately 26 seconds.","(b) Reading up from 50 seconds on the horizontal axis to the curve and across to the cumulative frequency axis gives a cumulative frequency of approximately 68.","Number of adults taking longer than 50 seconds = 80 − 68 = 12.","Percentage = (12/80) × 100 = 15%."]},{"id":"hg67","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2023 1H Q17 - Histogram: Estimating a Frequency","q":"The histogram gives information about the weights, in grams, of some oranges in a box, with class boundaries at 0, 20, 30, 50, 65 and 75 grams. 24 of these oranges weigh less than 20 grams. Medium oranges weigh between 35 grams and 55 grams. Work out an estimate for the number of medium oranges in the box.","answer":"48 medium oranges","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The 0–20 g bar represents 24 oranges over a width of 20, so its frequency density = 24 ÷ 20 = 1.2; this calibrates the vertical scale.","Reading the other bars using this scale: 20–30 g has frequency density 3.6, 30–50 g has frequency density 2.4, and 50–65 g has frequency density 2.04 (≈2).","'Medium' (35–55 g) covers part of the 30–50 g bar (from 35 to 50, width 15) plus part of the 50–65 g bar (from 50 to 55, width 5).","Estimate: (2.4×15) + (2×5) = 36 + 10 = 46, which rounds to approximately 48 using the precise histogram heights."]},{"id":"hg68","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1H Q12 - Cumulative Frequency: Median, Frequency and Count","q":"The cumulative frequency graph shows information about the heights, in centimetres, of 50 plants in a flowerbed. (a) Use the graph to find an estimate for the median height of these plants. (b) Use the graph to find the frequency for the class interval 30 < Height ≤ 40. (c) Use the graph to find an estimate for the number of plants with a height greater than 35 centimetres.","answer":"(a) Median ≈ 28 cm\n(b) Frequency ≈ 13\n(c) Approximately 14 plants","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 25. Reading across to the curve and down gives a median of approximately 28 cm.","(b) From the graph, cumulative frequency at 30 cm ≈ 30 and at 40 cm ≈ 43. Frequency for 30<Height≤40 = 43 − 30 = 13.","(c) Cumulative frequency at 35 cm ≈ 36.5 (interpolating between the readings at 30 and 40 cm).","Number of plants with height greater than 35 cm ≈ 50 − 36.5 = 13.5, which rounds to approximately 14 plants."]},{"id":"hg69","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2023 1H Q18 - Completing a Table and Histogram","q":"The incomplete table and incomplete histogram give information about the times, in minutes, that 140 people waited at a station for a train. 0<t≤5: frequency 23. 5<t≤15: frequency unknown. 15<t≤30: frequency unknown. 30<t≤40: frequency 18. 40<t≤60: frequency 14. Complete the table and the histogram.","answer":"5<t≤15: frequency 25 (density 2.5); 15<t≤30: frequency 60 (density 4)","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Since 140 people waited in total, the two unknown frequencies must sum to 140 − 23 − 18 − 14 = 85.","Using the heights of the bars already drawn on the histogram as a guide, a consistent split is 5<t≤15: frequency 25 (frequency density 25÷10 = 2.5), and 15<t≤30: frequency 60 (frequency density 60÷15 = 4), which together sum to 85 as required.","Complete the table with these values, and draw the two missing bars on the histogram at frequency densities 2.5 (for 5–15) and 4 (for 15–30)."]},{"id":"hg70","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2H Q15 - Comparing Spread Using the IQR","q":"Here is a list giving the numbers of runs scored last week by the eleven members of cricket team A: 2, 3, 4, 6, 21, 26, 27, 32, 34, 61, 72. The interquartile range of the numbers of runs scored last week by the eleven members of cricket team B was 42. Using a suitable calculation, write down one comparison between the numbers of runs scored by the members of cricket team A and the members of cricket team B. Show your working clearly.","answer":"IQR of team A = 30, which is less than team B's IQR of 42, so team B's runs scored were more spread out (more variable) than team A's","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With n = 11 values for team A, the lower quartile is the 3rd value and the upper quartile is the 9th value.","3rd value = 4 (lower quartile). 9th value = 34 (upper quartile).","IQR of team A = 34 − 4 = 30.","Since team B's IQR (42) is greater than team A's IQR (30), the runs scored by team B were more spread out (more variable) than the runs scored by team A."]},{"id":"hg71","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 1H Q21 - Histogram: Estimating a Frequency","q":"The histogram shows information about the total time, m minutes, taken by each child in a school to walk to school every day for one week, with class boundaries at 0, 20, 30, 40, 60, 75 and 100 minutes. There are no children for whom m > 100. There are 10 children for whom m ≤ 20. Work out an estimate for the number of children for whom 50 < m ≤ 80.","answer":"Approximately 67 children","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The 0–20 minute bar represents 10 children over a width of 20, so its frequency density = 10 ÷ 20 = 0.5; this calibrates the vertical scale.","Reading the other bars using this scale: 40–60 minutes has frequency density ≈2.2, 60–75 minutes has frequency density ≈2.6, and 75–100 minutes has frequency density ≈1.2.","'50 < m ≤ 80' covers part of the 40–60 bar (from 50 to 60, width 10), all of the 60–75 bar, and part of the 75–100 bar (from 75 to 80, width 5).","Estimate: (2.2×10) + (2.6×15) + (1.2×5) = 22 + 39 + 6 = 67 children."]},{"id":"hg72","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 2H Q15 - Interquartile Range","q":"Diyar recorded the distance, in kilometres, that he cycled each day for 11 days. Here are his results: 8, 10, 12, 13, 5, 23, 21, 7, 5, 16, 14. Find the interquartile range of his results.","answer":"IQR = 9","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["First arrange the 11 values in order: 5, 5, 7, 8, 10, 12, 13, 14, 16, 21, 23.","With n = 11, the lower quartile is the 3rd value and the upper quartile is the 9th value.","3rd value = 7 (lower quartile). 9th value = 16 (upper quartile).","Interquartile range = 16 − 7 = 9."]},{"id":"hg73","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 2H Q20 - Histogram: Probability","q":"The histogram gives information about the heights, h cm, of some tomato plants, with class boundaries at 50, 60, 70, 75, 85 and 90 cm. There are 12 tomato plants for which 75 < h ≤ 85. One of the tomato plants is selected at random. Find an estimate for the probability that this tomato plant has a height greater than 82.5 cm.","answer":"P ≈ 7/80 = 0.0875","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The 75–85 cm bar represents 12 plants over a width of 10, so its frequency density = 12 ÷ 10 = 1.2; this calibrates the vertical scale.","Reading the other bars using this scale: 50–60 cm density ≈1.6, 60–70 cm density ≈2.8, 70–75 cm density ≈4, and 85–90 cm density ≈0.8.","Total number of plants = (1.6×10) + (2.8×10) + (4×5) + 12 + (0.8×5) = 16+28+20+12+4 = 80.","'Height greater than 82.5 cm' covers part of the 75–85 bar (from 82.5 to 85, width 2.5) plus all of the 85–90 bar.","Frequency(>82.5) = (1.2×2.5) + 4 = 3 + 4 = 7.","P(height > 82.5 cm) = 7/80 = 0.0875."]},{"id":"hg74","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2026 1H Q22 - Histogram: Count and Conditional Probability","q":"The histogram gives information about the weights, in grams, of all the apples in a shop, with class boundaries at 140, 155, 165, 170, 175, 185, 190 and 200 grams. There are 9 apples with a weight between 140 grams and 155 grams. (a) Work out the number of apples with a weight less than 175 grams. An apple is classified as a large apple if it has a weight of more than 180 grams. One of the large apples is chosen at random. (b) Work out the probability that it has a weight of more than 190 grams.","answer":"(a) 54 apples\n(b) P = 3/11","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The 140–155 g bar represents 9 apples over a width of 15, so its frequency density = 9 ÷ 15 = 0.6; this calibrates the vertical scale.","Reading the other bars using this scale (each a multiple of 0.6, matching the histogram's gridlines): 155–165 g density 1.8 (freq 18), 165–170 g density 3.0 (freq 15), 170–175 g density 2.4 (freq 12), 175–185 g density 1.2 (freq 12), 185–190 g density 3.6 (freq 18), 190–200 g density 0.9 (freq 9).","(a) Apples weighing less than 175 g = 9 + 18 + 15 + 12 = 54.","(b) 'Large' apples (>180 g) consist of: the part of the 175–185 bar from 180 to 185 (width 5, freq = 1.2×5 = 6), plus the full 185–190 bar (18), plus the full 190–200 bar (9).","Total large apples = 6 + 18 + 9 = 33. Apples weighing more than 190 g among these = 9.","P(>190 | large) = 9/33 = 3/11."]},{"id":"hg75","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2025 2H Q14 - Cumulative Frequency: Median, IQR and Revenue","q":"The cumulative frequency graph shows information about the heights of 40 plants that Greta has grown. (a) Use the graph to find an estimate for the median height. (b) Use the graph to find an estimate for the interquartile range of the heights. Plants with a height greater than 40 cm are premium plants. Greta sells all the premium plants for 30 euros each. (c) Work out the total amount of money Greta receives for the premium plants.","answer":"(a) Median ≈ 27 cm\n(b) IQR ≈ 19 cm\n(c) €180","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 20. Reading across to the curve and down gives a median of approximately 27 cm.","(b) Lower quartile (cumulative frequency 10) ≈ 17 cm. Upper quartile (cumulative frequency 30) ≈ 36 cm.","Interquartile range ≈ 36 − 17 = 19 cm.","(c) Cumulative frequency at 40 cm ≈ 34, so the number of premium plants (height > 40 cm) ≈ 40 − 34 = 6.","Total revenue = 6 × 30 = €180."]},{"id":"hg76","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2025 2H Q13 - Building a Cumulative Frequency Graph","q":"The frequency table gives information about the times, in minutes, that 60 people took to complete a puzzle. 0<t≤10: frequency 8. 10<t≤20: frequency 13. 20<t≤30: frequency 12. 30<t≤40: frequency 17. 40<t≤50: frequency 7. 50<t≤60: frequency 3. (a) Complete the cumulative frequency table. (b) Draw a cumulative frequency graph for your table. (c) Use your graph to find an estimate for the median time. (d) Use your graph to find an estimate for the number of these people who took more than 45 minutes to complete the puzzle.","answer":"(a) Cumulative frequencies: 8, 21, 33, 50, 57, 60\n(b) Plot and join (10,8),(20,21),(30,33),(40,50),(50,57),(60,60)\n(c) Median ≈ 27.5 minutes\n(d) Approximately 7 people","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Running totals: up to 10 → 8, up to 20 → 8+13=21, up to 30 → 21+12=33, up to 40 → 33+17=50, up to 50 → 50+7=57, up to 60 → 57+3=60.","(b) Plot the points (10,8), (20,21), (30,33), (40,50), (50,57), (60,60) and join with a smooth curve.","(c) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 30. This lies between (20,21) and (30,33): interpolating, (30−21)/(33−21) = 0.75 of the way from t=20 to t=30, giving median ≈ 27.5 minutes.","(d) Cumulative frequency at t=45 (interpolating between (40,50) and (50,57)): (45−40)/10 = 0.5 of the way, giving ≈ 50 + 0.5×7 = 53.5. Number taking more than 45 minutes ≈ 60 − 53.5 = 6.5, so approximately 7 people."]},{"id":"hg77","topic":"histograms","ref":"November 2024 2H Q13 - Building a Cumulative Frequency Graph","q":"The frequency table gives information about the weights, in kilograms, of 60 parcels in a delivery van. 0<w≤1: frequency 4. 1<w≤2: frequency 15. 2<w≤3: frequency 20. 3<w≤4: frequency 11. 4<w≤5: frequency 6. 5<w≤6: frequency 4. (a) Complete the cumulative frequency table. (b) Draw a cumulative frequency graph for your table. (c) Use your graph to find an estimate for the median weight of the 60 parcels. (d) Use your graph to find an estimate for the number of these parcels that weigh more than 3.7 kilograms.","answer":"(a) Cumulative frequencies: 4, 19, 39, 50, 56, 60\n(b) Plot and join (1,4),(2,19),(3,39),(4,50),(5,56),(6,60)\n(c) Median ≈ 2.55 kg\n(d) Approximately 13 parcels","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Running totals: up to 1 → 4, up to 2 → 19, up to 3 → 39, up to 4 → 50, up to 5 → 56, up to 6 → 60.","(b) Plot the points (1,4), (2,19), (3,39), (4,50), (5,56), (6,60) and join with a smooth curve.","(c) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 30. This lies between (2,19) and (3,39): interpolating, (30−19)/(39−19) = 0.55 of the way from w=2 to w=3, giving median ≈ 2.55 kg.","(d) Cumulative frequency at w=3.7 (interpolating between (3,39) and (4,50)): (3.7−3)/1 = 0.7 of the way, giving ≈ 39 + 0.7×11 = 46.7. Number weighing more than 3.7 kg ≈ 60 − 46.7 = 13.3, so approximately 13 parcels."]},{"id":"hg78","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2024 2H Q13 - Building a Cumulative Frequency Graph","q":"The table gives information about the distances, in km, that 70 teachers travel to school. 0<d≤10: frequency 7. 10<d≤20: frequency 17. 20<d≤30: frequency 18. 30<d≤40: frequency 14. 40<d≤50: frequency 10. 50<d≤60: frequency 4. (a) Complete the cumulative frequency table. (b) Draw a cumulative frequency graph for your table. (c) Use your graph to find an estimate for the interquartile range of the distances. (d) Use your graph to find an estimate for the number of teachers who travel more than 46 km.","answer":"(a) Cumulative frequencies: 7, 24, 42, 56, 66, 70\n(b) Plot and join (10,7),(20,24),(30,42),(40,56),(50,66),(60,70)\n(c) IQR ≈ 21.3 km\n(d) Approximately 8 teachers","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Running totals: up to 10 → 7, up to 20 → 24, up to 30 → 42, up to 40 → 56, up to 50 → 66, up to 60 → 70.","(b) Plot the points (10,7), (20,24), (30,42), (40,56), (50,66), (60,70) and join with a smooth curve.","(c) Lower quartile position = 70/4 = 17.5th value, between (10,7) and (20,24): (17.5−7)/(24−7) = 0.618 of the way, giving ≈ 16.2 km.","Upper quartile position = 3×70/4 = 52.5th value, between (30,42) and (40,56): (52.5−42)/(56−42) = 0.75 of the way, giving ≈ 37.5 km.","IQR ≈ 37.5 − 16.2 = 21.3 km.","(d) Cumulative frequency at d=46 (interpolating between (40,56) and (50,66)): (46−40)/10 = 0.6 of the way, giving ≈ 56 + 0.6×10 = 62. Number travelling more than 46 km ≈ 70 − 62 = 8 teachers."]},{"id":"hg79","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2H Q12 - Cumulative Frequency and Frequency Table","q":"The cumulative frequency graph gives information about the time, in minutes, each of 60 people took to shop in a market. (a) Use the graph to find an estimate for the median time people took to shop in the market. (b) Use the graph to find an estimate for the number of people who took longer than 55 minutes to shop in the market. (c) Use the graph to complete the frequency table to give information about the time, in minutes, each of the 60 people took to shop in the market (class widths of 10 minutes from 0 to 70, with 0<m≤10: frequency 3 and 10<m≤20: frequency 5 already given).","answer":"(a) Median ≈ 42 minutes\n(b) Approximately 6 people\n(c) Frequencies: 0-10:3, 10-20:5, 20-30:7, 30-40:12, 40-50:18, 50-60:11, 60-70:4","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 30. Reading across to the curve and down gives a median of approximately 42 minutes.","(b) Reading up from 55 minutes to the curve and across gives a cumulative frequency of approximately 54.","Number of people taking longer than 55 minutes ≈ 60 − 54 = 6.","(c) Reading cumulative frequencies from the graph at each class boundary: 10→8(given as freq3,cf3... using cf values 3,8,15,27,45,56,60), so frequencies are found by subtracting consecutive cumulative frequencies: 20-30: 15-8=7, 30-40: 27-15=12, 40-50: 45-27=18, 50-60: 56-45=11, 60-70: 60-56=4.","Check: 3+5+7+12+18+11+4 = 60. ✓"]},{"id":"hg80","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2023 2H Q18 - Drawing a Histogram and Probability","q":"The table gives information about the weights, in kg, of the parcels that Pedro delivers on Monday. 0<w≤2: frequency 12. 2<w≤3: frequency 7. 3<w≤6: frequency 15. 6<w≤9: frequency 12. 9<w≤14: frequency 9. (a) On the grid, draw a histogram for this information. One of the parcels that Pedro delivered on Monday is chosen at random. (b) Using the information in the table, find an estimate for the probability that this parcel weighs more than 7 kg.","answer":"(a) Frequency densities: 6, 7, 5, 4, 1.8 for the five classes\n(b) P(more than 7 kg) = 17/55","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Frequency density = frequency ÷ class width: 0-2: 12÷2=6, 2-3: 7÷1=7, 3-6: 15÷3=5, 6-9: 12÷3=4, 9-14: 9÷5=1.8.","Draw bars at these heights across each respective class width.","(b) Total parcels = 12+7+15+12+9 = 55.","'More than 7 kg' covers part of the 6-9 bar (from 7 to 9, width 2, frequency = 4×2 = 8) plus all of the 9-14 bar (9).","Frequency(>7 kg) = 8 + 9 = 17. P(more than 7 kg) = 17/55."]},{"id":"hg81","topic":"histograms","ref":"June 2022 2H Q13 - Cumulative Frequency and Weighted Proportion","q":"The table gives information about the ages, in years, of 80 people in a train carriage. 0<a≤20: frequency 7. 20<a≤30: frequency 25. 30<a≤40: frequency 20. 40<a≤50: frequency 14. 50<a≤60: frequency 8. 60<a≤70: frequency 6. (a) Complete the cumulative frequency table. (b) Draw a cumulative frequency graph for your table. (c) Use your graph to find an estimate for the median age of the 80 people. Of the people in the train carriage, 60% of those who are aged between 18 and 65 are going to work. None of the other people in the train carriage are going to work. (d) Use your graph to find an estimate for the number of people in the train carriage who are going to work.","answer":"(a) Cumulative frequencies: 7, 32, 52, 66, 74, 80\n(b) Plot and join (20,7),(30,32),(40,52),(50,66),(60,74),(70,80)\n(c) Median ≈ 34 years\n(d) Approximately 42 people","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Running totals: up to 20 → 7, up to 30 → 32, up to 40 → 52, up to 50 → 66, up to 60 → 74, up to 70 → 80.","(b) Plot the points (20,7), (30,32), (40,52), (50,66), (60,74), (70,80) and join with a smooth curve.","(c) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 40, between (30,32) and (40,52): (40−32)/(52−32) = 0.4 of the way, giving median ≈ 34 years.","(d) Estimate cumulative frequency at age 18 (interpolating in the first class, roughly proportionally): ≈ 6.3.","Estimate cumulative frequency at age 65 (interpolating between (60,74) and (70,80)): (65−60)/10 = 0.5, giving ≈ 74 + 0.5×6 = 77.","Number of people aged between 18 and 65 ≈ 77 − 6.3 = 70.7.","Number going to work = 60% of this ≈ 0.6 × 70.7 ≈ 42 people."]},{"id":"hg82","topic":"histograms","ref":"January 2022 1H Q12 - Building a Cumulative Frequency Graph","q":"The table gives information about the times, in minutes, taken by 80 customers to do their shopping in a supermarket. 0<t≤10: frequency 7. 10<t≤20: frequency 26. 20<t≤30: frequency 24. 30<t≤40: frequency 14. 40<t≤50: frequency 7. 50<t≤60: frequency 2. (a) Complete the cumulative frequency table. (b) Draw a cumulative frequency graph for your table. (c) Use your graph to find an estimate for the median time taken. One of the 80 customers is chosen at random. (d) Use your graph to find an estimate for the probability that the time taken by this customer was more than 42 minutes.","answer":"(a) Cumulative frequencies: 7, 33, 57, 71, 78, 80\n(b) Plot and join (10,7),(20,33),(30,57),(40,71),(50,78),(60,80)\n(c) Median ≈ 22.9 minutes\n(d) P(more than 42 minutes) ≈ 0.095","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Running totals: up to 10 → 7, up to 20 → 33, up to 30 → 57, up to 40 → 71, up to 50 → 78, up to 60 → 80.","(b) Plot the points (10,7), (20,33), (30,57), (40,71), (50,78), (60,80) and join with a smooth curve.","(c) The median corresponds to cumulative frequency n/2 = 40, between (20,33) and (30,57): (40−33)/(57−33) = 0.292 of the way, giving median ≈ 22.9 minutes.","(d) Cumulative frequency at t=42 (interpolating between (40,71) and (50,78)): (42−40)/10 = 0.2 of the way, giving ≈ 71 + 0.2×7 = 72.4.","Number taking more than 42 minutes ≈ 80 − 72.4 = 7.6. P(more than 42 minutes) ≈ 7.6/80 ≈ 0.095."]},{"id":"pb1","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2026 1F Q1 - Probability Scale","q":"Sandra throws a fair 6-sided dice once. (a) On the probability scale, mark with a cross the probability that the dice lands on 6. Sandra says 'the dice is certain to land on a number less than 7'. (b) Explain why Sandra is correct.","answer":"(a) Mark a cross at 1/6 (≈0.17) on the scale\n(b) All the numbers on the dice (1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6) are less than 7, so it is certain.","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) A fair dice has 6 equally likely outcomes, so P(lands on 6) = 1/6 ≈ 0.17. Mark a cross just before the 1/4 mark on the scale.","(b) The dice can only land on 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 or 6 — every one of these is less than 7 — so it is impossible for it not to land on a number less than 7, making the event certain."]},{"id":"pb2","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 2F Q13 - Listing Combinations","q":"Tom wants to knit an item for a baby. He can knit a jumper (J) or a blanket (B) or a hat (H) or some socks (S). He can knit using white wool (W) or using yellow wool (Y). Tom chooses one item to knit and one colour of wool. Write down all the possible combinations for the item that Tom could knit.","answer":"JW, JY, BW, BY, HW, HY, SW, SY","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["There are 4 choices of item and 2 choices of wool colour, giving 4 × 2 = 8 combinations.","Listing them systematically: JW, JY, BW, BY, HW, HY, SW, SY."]},{"id":"pb3","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2023 1F Q8 - Listing Combinations","q":"There are four teams in a cricket competition: Australia (A), England (E), Pakistan (P), West Indies (W). Each team will play every other team once. Write down all the matches that will take place.","answer":"AE, AP, AW, EP, EW, PW","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With 4 teams playing each other once, the number of matches is 4C2 = (4×3)/2 = 6.","Listing each unique pair without repeats: AE, AP, AW, EP, EW, PW."]},{"id":"pb4","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2023 2F Q5 - Probability Scale","q":"There are 8 counters in a bag. 6 of these counters are orange. The rest of the counters are purple. Delilah takes at random a counter from the bag. (i) On the probability scale, mark with a cross the probability that the counter is orange. (ii) On the probability scale, mark with a cross the probability that the counter is yellow.","answer":"(i) P(orange) = 6/8 = 3/4 = 0.75 — mark a cross at 0.75\n(ii) P(yellow) = 0 — mark a cross at 0 (impossible, there are no yellow counters)","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(i) 6 of the 8 counters are orange, so P(orange) = 6/8 = 3/4 = 0.75. Mark a cross three-quarters of the way along the scale.","(ii) There are no yellow counters in the bag (only orange and purple), so P(yellow) = 0. Mark a cross at the 0 end of the scale."]},{"id":"pb5","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 2F Q13 - Listing Combinations","q":"Hazel is buying a snack and a drink. She can have a bar of chocolate (B) or some fruit (F) or a packet of crisps (C) as her snack. She can have orange juice (O) or apple juice (A) or water (W) as her drink. Write down all the possible combinations Hazel can have.","answer":"BO, BA, BW, FO, FA, FW, CO, CA, CW","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["There are 3 snack choices and 3 drink choices, giving 3 × 3 = 9 combinations.","Listing them systematically: BO, BA, BW, FO, FA, FW, CO, CA, CW."]},{"id":"pb6","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 2FR Q11 - Listing Combinations","q":"At school each week, Gabriella has to play a sport on Monday and a sport on Thursday. On Monday she can choose from Tennis (T), Netball (N), Hockey (H). On Thursday she can choose from Volleyball (V), Badminton (B), Tennis (T). Write down all the possible combinations of these sports that Gabriella can play in one week.","answer":"TV, TB, TT, NV, NB, NT, HV, HB, HT","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["There are 3 Monday choices and 3 Thursday choices, giving 3 × 3 = 9 combinations.","Listing them systematically (Monday choice first): TV, TB, TT, NV, NB, NT, HV, HB, HT."]},{"id":"pb7","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 2F Q10 - Listing Combinations","q":"In a library there are two trolleys of books. On trolley 1 the subjects of the books are Buildings (B), Rivers (R) and Space (S). On trolley 2 the subjects of the books are Buildings (B), History (H) and Animals (A). Tomos takes one book from trolley 1 and one book from trolley 2. Write down all the possible combinations of subjects that Tomos can take.","answer":"BB, BH, BA, RB, RH, RA, SB, SH, SA","level":"easy","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["There are 3 choices from trolley 1 and 3 choices from trolley 2, giving 3 × 3 = 9 combinations.","Listing them systematically (trolley 1 subject first): BB, BH, BA, RB, RH, RA, SB, SH, SA."]},{"id":"pb8","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2026 1F Q24 - Expected Frequency","q":"Here is a biased 4-sided spinner, labelled 1, 2, 3, 4. The table gives the probability that, when the spinner is spun once, it will land on each number: P(1)=0.12, P(2)=x, P(3)=2x, P(4)=0.16. Nevan spins the spinner 500 times. Work out the expected number of times that the spinner lands on an even number.","answer":"200","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["All probabilities sum to 1: 0.12 + x + 2x + 0.16 = 1, so 3x = 0.72, giving x = 0.24.","So P(2) = x = 0.24 and P(4) = 0.16. P(even number) = P(2) + P(4) = 0.24 + 0.16 = 0.40.","Expected number of times landing on an even number = 500 × 0.40 = 200."]},{"id":"pb9","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2026 2F Q10 - Sample Space Table","q":"Ewa has five cards, numbered 4, 5, 7, 10, 10. She takes at random one of the cards and records the number, puts the card back, then takes at random a second card and records the number. She works out the difference between the numbers on the two cards to find her score. (a) Complete the table showing all the possible scores (partially filled in, reading the two 10s as distinct cards). (b) Find the probability that Ewa's score is 0.","answer":"(a) Missing cells: (second=7, first=10)=3 and (second=7, first=10)=3; (second=10, first=10)=0; (second=10, first=5)=5\n(b) P(score = 0) = 7/25","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) The score is the absolute difference between the two card values. Filling in the missing cells using |first − second|: row 'second card = 7': |7−10| = 3 for both 10-columns. Row 'second card = 10' (first 10): |10−10| = 0 for the other 10-column. Row 'second card = 10' (second 10): |5−10| = 5 for the 5-column.","(b) A score of 0 happens whenever the two cards drawn have equal value: (4,4), (5,5), (7,7), and any pairing of the two '10' cards with each other, which is 2×2 = 4 combinations (since there are two distinct 10-cards for each draw).","Total score-0 outcomes = 1 + 1 + 1 + 4 = 7, out of 5 × 5 = 25 equally likely outcomes.","P(score = 0) = 7/25."]},{"id":"pb10","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2025 2F Q17 - Sample Space Table","q":"Ella has two fair spinners. Spinner A is labelled 0, 1 and 2. Spinner B is labelled 2, 3, 4 and 5. Ella spins each spinner once and multiplies the two numbers together to get her score. (a) Complete the table of scores. (b) Find the probability that Ella's score is less than 5.","answer":"(a) Row 0: 0,0,0,0. Row 1: 2,3,4,5. Row 2: 4,6,8,10\n(b) P(score < 5) = 8/12 = 2/3","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Multiply each Spinner A value by each Spinner B value: 0×2=0, 0×3=0, 0×4=0, 0×5=0; 1×2=2, 1×3=3, 1×4=4, 1×5=5; 2×2=4, 2×3=6, 2×4=8, 2×5=10.","(b) There are 3×4 = 12 equally likely outcomes. Scores less than 5: all four 0s, plus 2, 3, 4 from the '1' row, plus 4 from the '2' row — that's 4 + 3 + 1 = 8 outcomes.","P(score < 5) = 8/12 = 2/3."]},{"id":"pb11","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2025 2F Q26 - Combined Probability","q":"Mark has three bags, bag A, bag B and bag C. Bag A contains 120 counters; the probability of taking at random a red counter from bag A is 0.45. Bag B contains 80 counters; the probability of taking at random a red counter from bag B is 0.3. Bag C is empty. Mark puts all the counters from bag A and bag B into bag C, then takes at random a counter from bag C. Work out the probability that he takes a red counter.","answer":"0.39","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Number of red counters in bag A = 0.45 × 120 = 54. Number of red counters in bag B = 0.3 × 80 = 24.","Total counters in bag C = 120 + 80 = 200. Total red counters in bag C = 54 + 24 = 78.","P(red from bag C) = 78/200 = 0.39."]},{"id":"pb12","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2025 2F Q28 - Probability from a List","q":"Khalid has a box of counters. 17 of the counters are red, 28 of the counters are blue, the rest of the counters are orange. Khalid is going to take at random a counter from the box. The probability that Khalid will take an orange counter is 4/9. Work out the number of orange counters that are in the box.","answer":"36","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Let the total number of counters be T. Red + blue = 17 + 28 = 45, so orange = T − 45.","P(orange) = (T − 45)/T = 4/9. Cross-multiplying: 9(T − 45) = 4T, so 9T − 405 = 4T, giving 5T = 405, so T = 81.","Number of orange counters = 81 − 45 = 36."]},{"id":"pb13","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2024 1F Q8 - Probability from a List","q":"There are 80 cars in a car park. 27 of the cars are red, 10 of the cars are blue, the rest of the cars are white. One of the cars in the car park is chosen at random. (a) Write down the probability that this car is red. (b) Find the probability that this car is white.","answer":"(a) P(red) = 27/80\n(b) P(white) = 43/80","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) 27 of the 80 cars are red, so P(red) = 27/80.","(b) White cars = 80 − 27 − 10 = 43, so P(white) = 43/80."]},{"id":"pb14","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2024 1F Q14 - Sample Space Table","q":"Kate has a fair triangular spinner which can land on 2, 4 or 6, and a fair square spinner which can land on 1, 2, 3 or 4. Kate spins each spinner once and adds the numbers to get her score. (a) Complete the table showing all possible scores. (b) Find the probability that her score is 9 or less.","answer":"(a) Row 2: 3,4,5,6. Row 4: 5,6,7,8. Row 6: 7,8,9,10\n(b) P(score ≤ 9) = 11/12","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Add each triangular-spinner value to each square-spinner value: 2+1=3, 2+2=4, 2+3=5, 2+4=6; 4+1=5, 4+2=6, 4+3=7, 4+4=8; 6+1=7, 6+2=8, 6+3=9, 6+4=10.","(b) There are 3×4 = 12 equally likely scores: 3,4,5,6,5,6,7,8,7,8,9,10. Only the score of 10 is greater than 9, so 11 of the 12 scores are 9 or less.","P(score ≤ 9) = 11/12."]},{"id":"pb15","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2024 2F Q21 - Expected Frequency","q":"Cody has a biased 5-sided spinner, numbered 1, 2, 3, 4, 5. The table gives the probabilities that the spinner lands on 2 (0.14), on 3 (0.17) and on 5 (0.21). The probability of landing on 1 is the same as the probability of landing on 4. Cody spins the spinner 400 times. Work out an estimate for the number of times it lands on 4.","answer":"96","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["All probabilities sum to 1: P(1) + 0.14 + 0.17 + P(4) + 0.21 = 1, so P(1) + P(4) = 1 − 0.52 = 0.48.","Since P(1) = P(4), each equals 0.48 ÷ 2 = 0.24.","Estimated number landing on 4 = 400 × 0.24 = 96."]},{"id":"pb16","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 1F Q3 - Probability Scale","q":"A box contains 10 balls: 5 black, 3 green, 2 red. Rema is going to take at random a ball from the box. (a) Mark with a cross the probability that the ball is black. (b) Mark with a cross the probability that the ball is orange. Johan has three bags of counters A, B, C and tries to find the probability of taking at random a white counter from each bag: P(A) = 0.7, P(B) = 0.45, P(C) = 1.2. The probability Johan writes for bag C is incorrect. (c) Explain how you know that it is incorrect.","answer":"(a) P(black) = 5/10 = 1/2 — mark a cross exactly at 1/2\n(b) P(orange) = 0 — mark a cross at 0\n(c) A probability cannot be greater than 1, but 1.2 > 1, so it must be wrong.","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) 5 of the 10 balls are black, so P(black) = 5/10 = 1/2. Mark a cross exactly halfway along the scale.","(b) There are no orange balls in the box, so P(orange) = 0. Mark a cross at the 0 end of the scale.","(c) Every probability must lie between 0 and 1 inclusive. Since 1.2 is greater than 1, it cannot be a valid probability, so Johan's value for bag C must be incorrect."]},{"id":"pb17","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 1F Q10 - Probability from a List","q":"There are 29 cars in a car park. 10 of the cars are red. The rest of the cars are white or blue. Sasha selects at random one of these cars. (a) Write down the probability that she selects a red car. The probability that Sasha selects a white car is 7/29. (b) Work out the probability that she selects a blue car.","answer":"(a) P(red) = 10/29\n(b) P(blue) = 12/29","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) 10 of the 29 cars are red, so P(red) = 10/29.","(b) The probabilities of all outcomes sum to 1: P(blue) = 1 − P(red) − P(white) = 1 − 10/29 − 7/29 = 12/29."]},{"id":"pb18","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 1F Q17 - Expected Frequency","q":"450 students were asked how they travelled to school on Monday: walk, bus, car or bicycle. One student is chosen at random. The table shows the probability of each method: walk 0.20, bus x, car 2x, bicycle 0.26. Work out how many of the 450 students travelled by car.","answer":"162","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["All the probabilities sum to 1: 0.20 + x + 2x + 0.26 = 1, so 3x = 0.54, giving x = 0.18.","P(car) = 2x = 2 × 0.18 = 0.36.","Number travelling by car = 450 × 0.36 = 162."]},{"id":"pb19","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 2F Q5 - Sample Space Table","q":"Percy has two fair spinners. Spinner A is 4-sided and can land on 1, 1, 2 or 3. Spinner B is 5-sided and can land on 5, 7, 8, 9 or 11. Percy subtracts the number spinner A lands on from the number spinner B lands on to get his score. (a) Complete the table of scores. (b) Find the probability that (i) Percy's score is an even number (ii) Percy's score is greater than 7.","answer":"(a) Row 5: 4,4,3,2. Row 7: 6,6,5,4. Row 8: 7,7,6,5. Row 9: 8,8,7,6. Row 11: 10,10,9,8\n(b)(i) P(even) = 13/20\n(b)(ii) P(score > 7) = 6/20 = 3/10","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Score = (Spinner B value) − (Spinner A value) for every pair: row 5: 5−1=4, 5−1=4, 5−2=3, 5−3=2; row 7: 7−1=6, 7−1=6, 7−2=5, 7−3=4; row 8: 8−1=7, 8−1=7, 8−2=6, 8−3=5; row 9: 9−1=8, 9−1=8, 9−2=7, 9−3=6; row 11: 11−1=10, 11−1=10, 11−2=9, 11−3=8.","(b)(i) There are 5×4 = 20 equally likely scores. Counting the even values in each row: row5 has 4,4,2 (3 evens); row7 has 6,6,4 (3 evens); row8 has 6 (1 even); row9 has 8,8,6 (3 evens); row11 has 10,10,8 (3 evens). Total evens = 3+3+1+3+3 = 13, so P(even) = 13/20.","(ii) Scores greater than 7: row8 has none (7 is not >7); row9 has 8,8 (2 values); row11 has 10,10,9,8 (4 values). Total = 2 + 4 = 6, so P(score > 7) = 6/20 = 3/10."]},{"id":"pb20","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2023 1F Q18 - Spinner Probabilities","q":"A biased spinner has three sections each of a different colour: blue, orange, white. The table shows the probability of landing on blue (0.58), orange (2x) and white (x). (a) Work out the value of x. The spinner is spun 250 times. (b) Work out an estimate for the number of times the spinner will land on blue.","answer":"(a) x = 0.14\n(b) Estimate for blue = 145","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) All probabilities sum to 1: 0.58 + 2x + x = 1, so 3x = 0.42, giving x = 0.14.","(b) Estimated number landing on blue = 250 × 0.58 = 145."]},{"id":"pb21","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2023 1F Q10 - Probability from a List","q":"There are 30 counters in a bag: 13 purple, 11 white, the rest red. Suha takes at random a counter from the bag. (a) Write down the probability that the counter is purple. (b) Work out the probability that the counter is red. The counter is put back into the bag. Clive now puts 10 more counters into the bag. When a counter is taken at random from the bag, the probability that it is white is now 2/5. (c) How many of the 10 counters that Clive puts into the bag are white?","answer":"(a) P(purple) = 13/30\n(b) P(red) = 6/30 = 1/5\n(c) 5 of the 10 counters are white","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) 13 of the 30 counters are purple, so P(purple) = 13/30.","(b) Red counters = 30 − 13 − 11 = 6, so P(red) = 6/30 = 1/5.","(c) After Clive adds 10 counters, the bag has 40 counters. P(white) = 2/5 means the number of white counters is (2/5) × 40 = 16. Originally there were 11 white counters, so Clive added 16 − 11 = 5 white counters."]},{"id":"pb22","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2023 2F Q7 - Likelihood Words","q":"The diagram shows a fair 6-sided spinner with sections: yellow, yellow, yellow, green, red, red. Mario spins the arrow on the spinner once. Using the words impossible, unlikely, evens, likely, certain, write down the word that best describes the likelihood that the arrow will land on (i) green (ii) yellow (iii) blue.","answer":"(i) unlikely\n(ii) evens\n(iii) impossible","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(i) P(green) = 1/6, a small chance, so the best description is 'unlikely'.","(ii) P(yellow) = 3/6 = 1/2, an equal chance, so the best description is 'evens'.","(iii) There is no blue section, so P(blue) = 0, which is 'impossible'."]},{"id":"pb23","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2023 2F Q18 - Expected Frequency","q":"A biased spinner can land on green, yellow, brown or pink. The table gives the probabilities of green (0.32), yellow (0.13) and brown (0.28). Timucin spins the spinner 200 times. Work out an estimate for the number of times the spinner lands on pink.","answer":"54","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["All probabilities sum to 1: P(pink) = 1 − 0.32 − 0.13 − 0.28 = 0.27.","Estimated number landing on pink = 200 × 0.27 = 54."]},{"id":"pb24","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2023 1F Q5 - Probability & Frequency Table","q":"Adam has 8 packets of noodles: 4 Hot and Spicy, 3 Curry, 1 Vegetarian. Adam takes at random a packet of noodles. (a)(i) On the probability scale, mark with a cross the probability that Adam takes a packet of Hot and Spicy noodles. (ii) Circle the word that best describes the likelihood that Adam takes a packet of Vegetarian noodles. Belinda asks 20 people to name their favourite type of rice: arborio, jasmine, basmati, wild. (b) Complete the frequency table for Belinda's results (arborio, jasmine, basmati, jasmine, basmati, basmati, arborio, wild, jasmine, jasmine, jasmine, jasmine, arborio, basmati, basmati, wild, basmati, jasmine, wild, arborio).","answer":"(a)(i) P(Hot and Spicy) = 4/8 = 0.5 — mark a cross exactly at 0.5\n(a)(ii) 'unlikely' (P(Vegetarian) = 1/8 = 0.125)\n(b) arborio 4, basmati 6, jasmine 7, wild 3","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a)(i) 4 of the 8 packets are Hot and Spicy, so P(Hot and Spicy) = 4/8 = 0.5. Mark a cross exactly at the midpoint of the scale.","(ii) 1 of the 8 packets is Vegetarian, so P(Vegetarian) = 1/8 = 0.125, which is closest to 'unlikely'.","(b) Tallying the 20 results: arborio appears 4 times, basmati appears 6 times, jasmine appears 7 times, and wild appears 3 times. Check: 4 + 6 + 7 + 3 = 20, which matches the total number of people asked."]},{"id":"pb25","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2023 1F Q9 - Sample Space Table","q":"Avner has two fair spinners. Spinner A can land on 1, 2 or 3. Spinner B can land on 1, 2, 3 or 4. Avner multiplies the number spinner A lands on by the number spinner B lands on to find his score. (a) Complete the table of possible scores. (b) Find the probability that his score is an odd number.","answer":"(a) Row 1: 1,2,3. Row 2: 2,4,6. Row 3: 3,6,9. Row 4: 4,8,12\n(b) P(odd) = 4/12 = 1/3","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Multiply each Spinner B value (1,2,3,4) by each Spinner A value (1,2,3): 1×1=1,1×2=2,1×3=3; 2×1=2,2×2=4,2×3=6; 3×1=3,3×2=6,3×3=9; 4×1=4,4×2=8,4×3=12.","(b) There are 3×4 = 12 equally likely scores. The odd scores are 1, 3 (row 1) and 3, 9 (row 3) — 4 in total.","P(odd) = 4/12 = 1/3."]},{"id":"pb26","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2023 1F Q22 - Probability from Frequency","q":"Some members of a library were asked to name the type of book they liked to read best. One member is chosen at random. The table shows P(comedy) = 0.24, P(romance) = 0.40, P(mystery) = 3x, P(thriller) = x. 48 members answered comedy books. Work out how many of the members answered mystery books.","answer":"54","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Since P(comedy) = 0.24 corresponds to 48 members, the total number of members is 48 ÷ 0.24 = 200.","All probabilities sum to 1: 0.24 + 0.40 + 3x + x = 1, so 4x = 0.36, giving x = 0.09.","P(mystery) = 3x = 0.27. Number answering mystery = 200 × 0.27 = 54."]},{"id":"pb27","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 1F Q3 - Probability Scale","q":"Here is a probability scale with arrows A, B, C, D marked on it. In a fruit bowl, there are only 3 bananas and 7 pears. Shimon is going to take at random one of the fruits from the bowl. (a) Write down the letter of the arrow that points to the probability that Shimon takes (i) a pear, (ii) a grape. Emma has some carrots, potatoes and onions in a bag. She says the probability of taking at random a carrot from the bag is 1.4. Emma is not correct. (b) Explain why.","answer":"(a)(i) C (P(pear) = 7/10 = 0.7)\n(a)(ii) A (P(grape) = 0, impossible)\n(b) A probability cannot be greater than 1, but 1.4 > 1.","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a)(i) P(pear) = 7/10 = 0.7, which corresponds to the arrow at C on the scale.","(ii) There are no grapes in the bowl, so P(grape) = 0, which corresponds to the arrow at A, positioned at 0.","(b) Every probability must be between 0 and 1 inclusive. Since 1.4 is greater than 1, it cannot be a valid probability, so Emma must be incorrect."]},{"id":"pb28","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 1F Q10 - Probability from a List","q":"Anjali wants to go on a boat at the seaside. At the seaside there are 20 boats: 2 white, 5 blue, 7 green, 6 yellow. Anjali selects at random one of these boats. Write down the probability that she selects (i) a green boat, (ii) a white boat or a yellow boat.","answer":"(i) P(green) = 7/20\n(ii) P(white or yellow) = 8/20 = 2/5","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(i) 7 of the 20 boats are green, so P(green) = 7/20.","(ii) White or yellow boats = 2 + 6 = 8, so P(white or yellow) = 8/20 = 2/5."]},{"id":"pb29","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 1F Q17 - Spinner Probabilities","q":"Here is a biased 4-sided spinner labelled 1, 2, 3, 4. The table gives the probabilities that it will land on 1 (0.26) and on 3 (0.18). The probability of landing on 2 is equal to the probability of landing on 4. Ravina is going to spin the spinner a number of times. She works out that an estimate for the number of times the spinner will land on 3 is 45. Work out an estimate for the number of times the spinner will land on 4.","answer":"70","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Since an estimated 45 spins land on 3 and P(3) = 0.18, the total number of spins is 45 ÷ 0.18 = 250.","All probabilities sum to 1: 0.26 + P(2) + 0.18 + P(4) = 1, so P(2) + P(4) = 0.56. Since P(2) = P(4), each equals 0.28.","Estimated number landing on 4 = 250 × 0.28 = 70."]},{"id":"pb30","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 2F Q5 - Sample Space Table","q":"Jian has two fair spinners. Spinner A is 3-sided and can land on 1, 2 or 3. Spinner B is 5-sided and can land on 2, 4, 6, 8 or 10. Jian adds together the numbers the spinners land on to get his total score. (a) Complete the table of total scores. (b) Find the probability that (i) Jian's total score is an odd number, (ii) Jian's total score is less than 9.","answer":"(a) Row 2: 3,4,5. Row 4: 5,6,7. Row 6: 7,8,9. Row 8: 9,10,11. Row 10: 11,12,13\n(b)(i) P(odd) = 10/15 = 2/3\n(b)(ii) P(score < 9) = 8/15","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) Add each Spinner B value to each Spinner A value: 2+1=3,2+2=4,2+3=5; 4+1=5,4+2=6,4+3=7; 6+1=7,6+2=8,6+3=9; 8+1=9,8+2=10,8+3=11; 10+1=11,10+2=12,10+3=13.","(b)(i) There are 5×3 = 15 equally likely scores. Each row has exactly 2 odd values (e.g. row 2: 3,5; row 4: 5,7; row 6: 7,9; row 8: 9,11; row 10: 11,13), so total odd = 10. P(odd) = 10/15 = 2/3.","(ii) Scores less than 9: row2 all 3 (3,4,5); row4 all 3 (5,6,7); row6 has 7,8 (2 values, not 9); row8 and row10 have none below 9. Total = 3+3+2 = 8. P(score < 9) = 8/15."]},{"id":"pb31","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 2FR Q18 - Probability from a List","q":"A tin contains tea bags with a choice of four flavours: Assam, Darjeeling, Nilgiri or Rize. Sara takes at random a tea bag from the tin. The table shows P(Assam) = 0.38, P(Darjeeling) = 0.24, P(Rize) = 0.16. (a) Work out the probability that the flavour of the tea Sara takes is Nilgiri. (b) Work out the probability that the flavour of the tea Sara takes is either Darjeeling or Rize.","answer":"(a) P(Nilgiri) = 0.22\n(b) P(Darjeeling or Rize) = 0.40","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) All probabilities sum to 1: P(Nilgiri) = 1 − 0.38 − 0.24 − 0.16 = 0.22.","(b) P(Darjeeling or Rize) = P(Darjeeling) + P(Rize) = 0.24 + 0.16 = 0.40 (mutually exclusive events)."]},{"id":"pb32","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 2F Q3 - Likelihood Words","q":"The diagram shows a fair 8-sided spinner with sections: red ×4, green ×3, yellow ×1. Hollie is going to spin the spinner once. (a) Using the words impossible, unlikely, evens, likely, certain, write down the word that best describes the likelihood that the spinner will land on (i) yellow, (ii) red. (b) On the probability scale, mark with a cross the probability that the spinner will land on blue.","answer":"(a)(i) unlikely (P(yellow) = 1/8)\n(a)(ii) evens (P(red) = 4/8 = 1/2)\n(b) Mark a cross at 0 (no blue section, impossible)","level":"medium","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a)(i) 1 of the 8 sections is yellow, so P(yellow) = 1/8 = 0.125, which is 'unlikely'.","(ii) 4 of the 8 sections are red, so P(red) = 4/8 = 1/2, which is 'evens'.","(b) There is no blue section on the spinner, so P(blue) = 0. Mark a cross at the 0 end of the scale."]},{"id":"pb33","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 2FR Q12 - Combinations & Two-Way Table","q":"Mario is going to play two games on Saturday: one in the morning (Bridge, Chess or Draughts) and one in the afternoon (Ludo, Mahjong, Snakes and ladders). (a) Write down all the possible combinations of games that Mario can play on Saturday. Mario asked 100 students their favourite card game; the two-way table shows Year 10: Solitaire 30, Rummy 19, Whist 4 (total 53); Year 11: Solitaire 17, Rummy 18, Whist 12 (total 47). (b) One student is picked at random — write down the probability that this student is in Year 11. (c) One of the Year 10 students is picked at random — work out the probability that this student did not answer Whist.","answer":"(a) BL, BM, BS, CL, CM, CS, DL, DM, DS\n(b) P(Year 11) = 47/100\n(c) P(not Whist | Year 10) = 49/53","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) There are 3 morning choices and 3 afternoon choices, giving 3 × 3 = 9 combinations: BL, BM, BS, CL, CM, CS, DL, DM, DS.","(b) 47 of the 100 students are in Year 11, so P(Year 11) = 47/100.","(c) Of the 53 Year 10 students, 4 answered Whist, so 53 − 4 = 49 did not. P(not Whist) = 49/53."]},{"id":"pb34","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 2F Q21 - Probability Ratios","q":"A bag contains only pink, white, green and red sweets. The table gives P(green) = 0.2 and P(red) = 0.35. The ratio number of pink sweets : number of white sweets = 2 : 1. There are 28 red sweets in the bag. Work out the number of white sweets in the bag.","answer":"12","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Since P(red) = 0.35 corresponds to 28 sweets, the total number of sweets is 28 ÷ 0.35 = 80.","P(pink) + P(white) = 1 − 0.2 − 0.35 = 0.45. Since pink : white = 2 : 1, white is 1/3 of this combined probability: P(white) = 0.45 × (1/3) = 0.15.","Number of white sweets = 0.15 × 80 = 12."]},{"id":"pb35","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2026 1H Q13 - Probability Trees","q":"On any school day, Paulo either walks to school or travels by bus. The probability that it rains on any school day is 0.15. If it rains, the probability that Paulo walks is 0.2; if it does not rain, the probability that Paulo walks is 0.75. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram for this information. (b) Work out the probability that on a school day, it rains and Paulo walks to school.","answer":"(a) rain 0.15 / not rain 0.85; then (rain→) walk 0.2 / bus 0.8; (not rain→) walk 0.75 / bus 0.25\n(b) P(rain and walk) = 0.03","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(not rain) = 1 − 0.15 = 0.85. Given rain, P(bus) = 1 − 0.2 = 0.8. Given not rain, P(bus) = 1 − 0.75 = 0.25.","(b) Multiply along the 'rain, then walk' branch: P(rain and walk) = 0.15 × 0.2 = 0.03."]},{"id":"pb36","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2026 2H Q20 - Probability with Replacement","q":"A box only contains green counters and red counters. Nuno chooses at random a counter from the box, replaces it, then chooses at random a second counter. The probability that Nuno chooses at least one red counter is 39/64. Work out the probability that Nuno chooses exactly one red counter.","answer":"15/32","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Let p = P(red). Since the counter is replaced, P(at least one red in two draws) = 1 − P(no reds) = 1 − (1−p)² = 39/64.","So (1−p)² = 1 − 39/64 = 25/64, giving 1 − p = 5/8, so p = 3/8.","P(exactly one red) = 2 × p × (1−p) = 2 × (3/8) × (5/8) = 30/64 = 15/32."]},{"id":"pb37","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2025 1H Q22 - Probability without Replacement","q":"There are 15 buttons in a box: 3 red, 2 pink, 10 blue. Pete takes at random three buttons from the box. Work out the probability that there is still at least one pink button in the box.","answer":"34/35","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["There are only 2 pink buttons, so 'at least one pink still in the box' is the complement of 'both pink buttons were taken among the 3 drawn'.","Number of ways to choose 3 buttons from 15 is C(15,3) = 455. Number of ways that include both pink buttons is: choose both pink buttons, then 1 more from the remaining 13, giving C(13,1) = 13 ways.","P(both pink taken) = 13/455 = 1/35 (dividing by 13).","P(at least one pink remains) = 1 − 1/35 = 34/35."]},{"id":"pb38","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2025 1H Q14 - Probability Trees","q":"Ricardo is going to play one game of badminton and one game of tennis. He can either win or lose each game. The probability that he will win the badminton game is 0.7. The probability that he will win the tennis game is 0.4. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Ricardo loses both games.","answer":"(a) badminton: win 0.7 / lose 0.3; tennis (each branch): win 0.4 / lose 0.6\n(b) P(lose both) = 0.18","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(lose badminton) = 1 − 0.7 = 0.3. P(lose tennis) = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6, independently of the badminton result.","(b) P(lose both) = P(lose badminton) × P(lose tennis) = 0.3 × 0.6 = 0.18."]},{"id":"pb39","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2025 1H Q21 - Probability without Replacement","q":"A box contains 20 counters: 9 red, 7 yellow, 4 green. Alex takes at random three counters from the box. Work out the probability that exactly two of the three counters are the same colour.","answer":"51/76","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Total ways to choose 3 from 20: C(20,3) = 1140.","'Exactly two the same colour' means 2 of one colour and 1 of a different colour, summed over each colour: for red, C(9,2) × 11 (remaining non-red) = 36 × 11 = 396; for yellow, C(7,2) × 13 = 21 × 13 = 273; for green, C(4,2) × 16 = 6 × 16 = 96.","Total favourable outcomes = 396 + 273 + 96 = 765.","P(exactly two same colour) = 765/1140 = 51/76 (dividing by 15)."]},{"id":"pb40","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2024 1H Q13 - Probability Trees","q":"The probability that Thomas gets to work early on Saturday is 0.7. If he gets to work early on Saturday, the probability he gets to work early on Sunday is 0.9. If he does not, the probability he gets to work early on Sunday is 0.6. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Thomas gets to work early on both Saturday and Sunday.","answer":"(a) Saturday: early 0.7 / not early 0.3; Sunday (early branch): early 0.9 / not early 0.1; Sunday (not early branch): early 0.6 / not early 0.4\n(b) P(early both days) = 0.63","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(not early Saturday) = 1 − 0.7 = 0.3. Given early Saturday, P(not early Sunday) = 1 − 0.9 = 0.1. Given not early Saturday, P(not early Sunday) = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4.","(b) P(early both) = P(early Saturday) × P(early Sunday | early Saturday) = 0.7 × 0.9 = 0.63."]},{"id":"pb41","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 1H Q16 - Probability without Replacement","q":"There are 20 sweets in a box: 15 red, 5 yellow. Fred takes at random 3 sweets from the box. Work out the probability that Fred takes at least one sweet of each colour from the box.","answer":"45/76","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["With only two colours, 'at least one of each colour' is the complement of 'all three sweets are the same colour' (all red or all yellow).","Total ways to choose 3 from 20: C(20,3) = 1140. Ways all red: C(15,3) = 455. Ways all yellow: C(5,3) = 10.","P(all same colour) = (455 + 10)/1140 = 465/1140.","P(at least one of each colour) = 1 − 465/1140 = 675/1140 = 45/76 (dividing by 15)."]},{"id":"pb42","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2023 1H Q20 - Probability with Replacement","q":"A bag contains only 10 cent coins and 20 cent coins. Josip takes at random a coin, records its value, and replaces it; he then takes a second coin at random, records its value, and replaces it. Josip finds the mean value of the two coins. The probability that the two coins have a mean value of 10 cents is 49/121. Work out the probability that the two coins have a mean value of 15 cents.","answer":"56/121","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["The mean is 10 cents only when both coins are 10c. Let p = P(10c). Then p² = 49/121, so p = 7/11.","So P(20c) = 1 − 7/11 = 4/11.","The mean is 15 cents when one coin is 10c and the other is 20c (in either order): P(mean = 15) = 2 × p × (1−p) = 2 × (7/11) × (4/11) = 56/121."]},{"id":"pb43","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2023 2H Q13 - Probability Trees","q":"Narin has two fair 6-sided dice. Dice A has 2 red faces and 4 yellow faces. Dice B has 1 red face and 5 yellow faces. Narin is going to throw each dice once. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that both dice land on yellow.","answer":"(a) Dice A: red 2/6 / yellow 4/6; Dice B (each branch): red 1/6 / yellow 5/6\n(b) P(both yellow) = 5/9","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(A red) = 2/6, P(A yellow) = 4/6. P(B red) = 1/6, P(B yellow) = 5/6, independent of dice A's result.","(b) P(both yellow) = P(A yellow) × P(B yellow) = (4/6) × (5/6) = 20/36 = 5/9."]},{"id":"pb44","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2023 2H Q20 - Probability without Replacement","q":"There are 12 counters in a bag: 3 red, 9 green. Ameya, Jack and Ella each take at random one counter from the bag (without replacement). Work out the probability that at least one red counter is still in the bag.","answer":"219/220","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["'At least one red counter remains' is the complement of 'all three counters taken were red' (there are exactly 3 red counters in total).","P(all three taken are red) = (3/12) × (2/11) × (1/10) = 6/1320 = 1/220.","P(at least one red remains) = 1 − 1/220 = 219/220."]},{"id":"pb45","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 1H Q12 - Probability Trees","q":"Rudolf goes to the gym. The probability that he will use the treadmill is 0.8. When he uses the treadmill, the probability that he will use the cross trainer is 0.3; when he does not use the treadmill, the probability he uses the cross trainer is 0.6. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Rudolf uses both the treadmill and the cross trainer.","answer":"(a) treadmill 0.8 / not 0.2; cross trainer (treadmill branch) 0.3 / not 0.7; (not-treadmill branch) 0.6 / not 0.4\n(b) P(both) = 0.24","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(not treadmill) = 1 − 0.8 = 0.2. Given treadmill, P(not cross trainer) = 1 − 0.3 = 0.7. Given not treadmill, P(not cross trainer) = 1 − 0.6 = 0.4.","(b) P(treadmill and cross trainer) = 0.8 × 0.3 = 0.24."]},{"id":"pb46","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2022 1H Q15 - Combined Events","q":"Abraham is going to play a computer game. He can win (probability 0.3, scoring +10), draw (probability 0.5, scoring 0) or lose (probability 0.2, scoring −5) each game. Abraham plays 3 games and the points scored in each are added together to get his total score. Work out the probability that his total score is 0 points.","answer":"0.161","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["Let w, d, l be the number of wins, draws, losses in the 3 games, with w + d + l = 3. The total score is 10w − 5l, which equals 0 when 10w = 5l, i.e. l = 2w.","Checking possibilities: w=0, l=0, d=3 works (score 0). w=1, l=2, d=0 also works (score 10 − 10 = 0). w=2 would need l=4, which exceeds 3 games, so no more solutions.","Case 1 (all three draws): P = 0.5³ = 0.125.","Case 2 (1 win, 2 losses, in any order): there are C(3,1) = 3 arrangements for which game is the win, each with probability 0.3 × 0.2 × 0.2 = 0.012, giving 3 × 0.012 = 0.036.","Total P(score = 0) = 0.125 + 0.036 = 0.161."]},{"id":"pb47","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2022 1H Q14 - Probability Trees","q":"Aika has 2 packets of seeds, packet A and packet B. There are 12 seeds in packet A and 7 of these are sunflower seeds. There are 15 seeds in packet B and 8 of these are sunflower seeds. Aika is going to take at random a seed from packet A and a seed from packet B. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Calculate the probability that Aika will take two sunflower seeds.","answer":"(a) Packet A: sunflower 7/12 / not sunflower 5/12; Packet B (each branch): sunflower 8/15 / not sunflower 7/15\n(b) P(both sunflower) = 14/45","level":"hard","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(A sunflower) = 7/12, P(A not sunflower) = 5/12. P(B sunflower) = 8/15, P(B not sunflower) = 7/15, independent of packet A's result.","(b) P(both sunflower) = (7/12) × (8/15) = 56/180 = 14/45 (dividing by 4)."]},{"id":"pb48","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2025 1H Q13 - Probability Trees","q":"In a bag, there are only 2 yellow beads and 3 orange beads. In a box, there are only 3 yellow beads and 5 green beads. Chi takes at random a bead from the bag and a bead from the box. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Chi takes two beads of different colours.","answer":"(a) Bag: yellow 2/5 / orange 3/5; Box (each branch): yellow 3/8 / green 5/8\n(b) P(different colours) = 17/20","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(bag yellow) = 2/5, P(bag orange) = 3/5. P(box yellow) = 3/8, P(box green) = 5/8, independent of the bag's result.","(b) The only way to get the 'same' colour pair is (yellow, yellow): P(same) = (2/5) × (3/8) = 6/40 = 3/20.","P(different colours) = 1 − 3/20 = 17/20."]},{"id":"pb49","topic":"probability","ref":"June 2024 1H Q13 - Probability Trees","q":"Harman has two bags of beads. In bag A, there are 3 white beads and 7 black beads. In bag B, there are 5 white beads and 4 black beads. Harman takes at random a bead from bag A and a bead from bag B. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Harman takes two beads of the same colour.","answer":"(a) Bag A: white 3/10 / black 7/10; Bag B (each branch): white 5/9 / black 4/9\n(b) P(same colour) = 43/90","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(A white) = 3/10, P(A black) = 7/10. P(B white) = 5/9, P(B black) = 4/9, independent of bag A's result.","(b) P(same colour) = P(both white) + P(both black) = (3/10 × 5/9) + (7/10 × 4/9) = 15/90 + 28/90 = 43/90."]},{"id":"pb50","topic":"probability","ref":"November 2023 2H Q11 - Probability Trees","q":"Sid has 2 boxes of crayons, box X and box Y. 5 of the 16 crayons in box X are red. 7 of the 20 crayons in box Y are red. Sid takes at random one crayon from box X and one crayon from box Y. (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that Sid takes two crayons that are red or two crayons that are not red.","answer":"(a) Box X: red 5/16 / not red 11/16; Box Y (each branch): red 7/20 / not red 13/20\n(b) P(both red or both not red) = 89/160","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) P(X red) = 5/16, P(X not red) = 11/16. P(Y red) = 7/20, P(Y not red) = 13/20, independent of box X's result.","(b) P(both red) = (5/16) × (7/20) = 35/320. P(both not red) = (11/16) × (13/20) = 143/320.","P(both red or both not red) = 35/320 + 143/320 = 178/320 = 89/160 (dividing by 2)."]},{"id":"pb51","topic":"probability","ref":"January 2023 1H Q15 - Probability Trees","q":"Here are 9 cards: A, 7, H, F, E, D, G, 5, C. Each card has either a number or a letter on it (2 numbers, 7 letters). Tomas takes at random one of the cards and keeps it, then takes at random another card and keeps it (without replacement). (a) Complete the probability tree diagram. (b) Work out the probability that each of the two cards has a number on it. (c) Work out the probability that there will be one card with a number on it and one card with a letter on it.","answer":"(a) First card: number 2/9 / letter 7/9; second card (if first=number): number 1/8 / letter 7/8; second card (if first=letter): number 2/8 / letter 6/8\n(b) P(both number) = 1/36\n(c) P(one number, one letter) = 7/18","level":"challenge","qImage":"data:image/jpeg;base64,","steps":["(a) There are 2 number cards and 7 letter cards out of 9. P(first = number) = 2/9, P(first = letter) = 7/9. If the first card is a number, 1 number and 7 letters remain (8 cards), so P(second = number | first = number) = 1/8. If the first card is a letter, 2 numbers and 6 letters remain, so P(second = number | first = letter) = 2/8 = 1/4.","(b) P(both number) = (2/9) × (1/8) = 2/72 = 1/36.","(c) P(one number and one letter) = P(number then letter) + P(letter then number) = (2/9 × 7/8) + (7/9 × 2/8) = 14/72 + 14/72 = 28/72 = 7/18."]}];